        <p>WMIEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FATALITIES.</p>
        <p>A Record Year in 1905.</p>
        <p>Norris Castle.—Norris Castle in the Isle of Wight, which it is thought King Alfonso may buy or lease as During 1905 1,099 persons in this country wetie a summer residence during the yachting season, is a and 0,-159 injured by accidents due to the running of romantic-looking ivy-mantled Castle, whose lawns and trains or the movement of railway vehicles." The wooded slopes adjoin the Osborne demesne. It was average figures for the previous nine yeat were 1,149 /Or a time the home of our late Queen Victoria, and 6,6a1 respectively. in her childhood, who, in her early married life tried hard to The year was remarkable for the number of fatalities purchase the place as a Royal residence. As the Duchess to passengers in train aceidents—raore indeed were of Bedford could not be prevailed upon to give it up, killed in this way than in any year since 1589, but 38 Osborne House was bought and re-built, but it is not so picturesque, as viewed from the sea, as is Norris out of a total of 39 deaths of passengers were attri- Castle. For butable to four collisions and derailments., a long time it xfas tenanted by Mr. Orr The number of passengers injured in train accidents Ewing, with whose little boys and girls, Princess Ens I , was, however, much elow the average, aiad, looking at and her brothers were great friends when they were i the figures for runny years, there appears to be DO rein. children.. More recently Norris Castle was occupied tion between the numbers killed and injured from this by Mr. and Mrs. John Morgan Richards, who have cause. recently suffered such a terrible bereavement in the The number f railway servants killed and injured in sudden death of their daughter, Mrs. Craigie.—Mr. and : train ..ccidents were six and twelve respectively, Mrs. Richards now reside at Steephill Castle at Ventnor, . figures far below the average of previous ,pars. where Mrs. Craigie spent a good deal of her time in 1 summer, and it was on the morrow of a return from I visit there, that this gifted playwright and novelist was I found in her room. "John Oliver Hobbes."—Among the unfinished works left by the late Mrs. Craigie, is a novel which was to have been called "A Time to Love,"—and had this brilliant authoress lived another week or two, she would have witnessed the publication of her new book.entitled "The Dream and the Business "—She adopted the masculine pen-name" John Oliver Hobbes " because— as she thought—it would be an advantage to her in , literature, but, from the time she produced her first serious work "Some Emotions and a Moral," in 1891, her place was assured in the front MIA of fiction writers. She was then only twenty-four, and for the past ,fifteen -years was one of the most industrious women of her day, for in addition to her writings, lectures and rehearsals of her plays, she was in great demand in the social world, and went a great deal into society. She married, when she was nineteen, but it was not a happy marriage,and after a few years she divorced her husband. To her only son, now in his sixteenth year, she was one of the most devoted of mothers,and it was her adoration for him that, in part, induced her to refuse many offers of a second marriage. The American Woman.—Although her parents have been settled in London for over' thirty years, the late Mrs. Craigie was an American by birth, and she knew her native country very well. It was only recently that she returned from a lecturing. tour in the States, and it was her opinion that American women were ahead of their sisters on this side of the Atlantic in general intelligence and education. She had no sympathy for women's rights and the suffrage, but she had a very high esteem .for the average American woman, who, however, is very often found to take a greater interest in public affairs than in her husband's business. That she regards as his concern, which will progress all right as a matter of course, whilst she is free to devote herself to the larger affairs of her country. She does not believe that women were meant to be like men, bnt that they were meant to be better, and the men of America believe it. One observes this among the many American holiday makers who visit us in the summer time. The women are all independent, frank, and self-possessed, the only nonentity being the man of the party. A Ducal Authoress.—The new Duchess of Rutland, so long known in Society as Lady Granby,has a recognised talent for literature, and a few years ago she published "Portraits of Men and %Verne n." She is the datighter of the late Hon. Charles Hugh Lindsay, who was so long and intimately connected with the Court of Queen Victoria and another literary member of her family was Lady Annie Barnard, the authoress of "Auld Robin Gray." The Duchess is, of course, a Scotswoman as was also the late Duchess of Rutland. add it is remarkable how man 3 of the Dukes have sought their wives in Scotland. The Duchess of Sutherland. the Duchess of Norfolk, the Duchess of Somerset. the Duchess of Buckingham and the Duchess of Northumberland,are all Scotswomen whilst the Duchess of Portland, and the Duchess of of Buccleuch have a good deal of Scots blood in them. Lady Townsbend's Poems.—The Marchioness Towns- bend could not have chosen a better time for the pro- duction of her volume of poems entitled "In the King's garden. and other Poems" published by Mr. John Long. The Marchioness had never aspired to fame in the literary world, and her poems, written at irregular intervals, were not originally intended for publication, but she has been persuaded to give them to the world in a volume adorned with an excellent photogravure portrait of the authoress. The Marchioness and her husband are now on the continent, seeking rest and seclusion after the trial which has recently brought their family life and affairs so prominently before the public. Colour Combinations.—The young Queen of Spain remains faithful to her favourite combination of blue and white, and 03 a consequence little else save blue and white was seen at Cowes. Taken as a whole, this particular combination is a happy one, and more becom- ing than many that have fallen to our lot. The modish heliotrope and white for example, of a few seasons back proved very, trying to the majority of com- plexions, while the somewhat brief craze for violet and blue.resulted in us all looking our very worst. During the week that has just gone by many new autumn shades have made their agpearance, and, as might be expected after the great vogue blue has lately enjoyed, this colour is not among them. Brown predominates, in cinnamon, marron, tabac, and wood-brown tints; rim olive greens, come next, and then greys. The notion that gray is unbecoming to all save those women and girls possessed of vivid colouring, has been proved to be a mistaken one, and the secret in rendering it a en:Tess to almost everyone, lies in the touch of some relieving colour which is applied to the collar-band, vest, sleeves, and waistbelt. The Spanish Mantilla.—An innovation at Cowes was the Spanish mantilla which was worn by several well- known Society beauties in the Squadron Gardens in the afternoon. The procedure was of course intended as a • compliment to Queen Victoria Eugenie, but the fact however unwelcome remains that our country women have yet to master the art of wearing the mantilla successfully. Princess Henry of Pleas, whose toilettes were the admiration—and perchance the envy—of all beholders was seen upon, several occasions with a lace drapery upon her beautifully arranged hair, indeed , during the greater part of the week, the Princess adapted many Spanish modes to her own particular style of beauty, and her ideas will of course, be quickly iollovred by all the important modistes.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE SOLDIER KILLEp IN AFRICA. News was received by Mr. Egan. of New-street,North- ,wich, on Monday night that his son, Private Pettc Egan, of the 3rd Batt. Manchester Regiment, had been lilted in South Africa. He was driving a Cape cart when one of the mules became obstinate. Deceased leaned forward to readjust the harness, when the .animals bolted, and he was thrown from the vehicle and run over. He died two days later.</p>
