        <head>.fiuthor of" The Doctor," "Fanny Lambert," etc.</head>
        <p>.fiuthor of" The Doctor," "Fanny Lambert," etc.</p>
        <p>1 " Yon Will come the day after to-morrow in the evening, then ? " had said Daisy "Yes, at eight o'clock; but why not to' morrow ? " ; "Because I am going to St: James's Hall with aunt 4 There's a meeting of the anti- something .society--cannibals, I think—and I must go: Bother the cannibals." " How am I to last all that time without seeing or hearing from you ? " "Aft what time, you stupid boxy?" "Till the day after to-morrow evening." "I'll tell you, Pippy "—she called him Pippy for some reason best known to herself— I'll write." " When ? - "I'll write you a letter to-morrow, so that you'll have it at breakfast the, day after to- morrow morning." "Promise me." "I promise you." How little they knew of all that was to take place on the day after Ad-morrow, unconscious of danger as children chasing butterflies on the edge of a cliff, or plucking flowers by the cave of a lion, they sealed their compact with a kiss and parted: On the day, the fateful day which saw Arthur Esmond trepanned and robbed. Daisy was filled with a pleasurable nervousness begotten of the anticipation of Arthur's visit and dread of the manner in which it was to be made. - She had been unfortunate all day. Coining • down to breakfast she had trodden on Fido's foot, and the foot of Fido, most hideous of all :lapdogs, was of more value in the eyes of Miss Princeps than the body of anyone else. She had hemmed a piece of lace and spoiled it, ,• and, in fact, everything she put her hand to went wrong. She had never told her aunt about Arthur, and this troubled here Naturally truthful, it. we,nt against her grain to act falsely, and yet, dealing with a person like Patience Princeps, to act faithfully would, in the eyes of a lover at least, ha.i,e been sheer lunacy. At seven o'clock she left the house. It was a beautiful evening in the garden and one might" have fancied oneself miles deep in the country instead of, so to speak, a stone's throw from 'the mighty city. She took the walk leading by the wall which separated the garden, from the lane. klere; sheltered by the laurels and trees. she could walk unobserved by anyone in the house: as she passed up and down she measured the wall with her eye. It was not really much of a wall to get over, and as scarcely anyone ever used the lane, the feat •of burglarionsly entering the garden was a very minor one. But in the innocent 'eyes of Daisy Graham it was heroic. Seven passed, and half-past seven. A church clock struck eight. Daisy stopped in her walk; at any moment now he might come. Quarter past eight—half-past eight. She looked at the watch on her wrist to make sure ; there could be no doubt, it was half-past eight, and 'he had not come. A flush came over her face. Could he' have offered her a greater insult than to have made an ap- pointment and failed to keep it ? She waited patiently, but with compressed lips till:nine o'clock. Nine o'clock struck and she returned to the house. Miss Princeps was in a tantrum. She was lying on the sofa with a fit of neuralgia, sniffing a scent-bottle and nursing Fido. She told Daisy to sit down and read to her, and the lamp being lit , Daisy sat clown and began to read. She had taken up a book at +random, and the sense of what she read was as lost to her as if it had been written in Hebrew. The dastardly conduct of Arthur Esmond had de-magnetised her mind ; the idea never occurred to her that any accident had be- fallen him, her only idea was that he was false. The clock pointed to quarter to ten as Le door opened and Parker, the maid, entered with a card on a salver. " A gentleman to see you. Miss," said ,Parker, presenting. the salver to Daisy. The girl took it and read: '.' MR. A. ROWLANDS. " London and, Lancashire Bank." "Show the gentleman in," said Daisy, little dreaming of the dragon she was about to usher into the presence of her aunt.</p>
        <p>and returned</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VII. THE servant withdrew ushering in the bank manaiier. Mr. Rowlands looked at the two ladies before him, from the younger to the elder and back again. I' He was a man of the world, and he took ir the situation at a glance ; he took in _Miss</p>
        <p>Princeps' character at once. He bowed tc her politely and turning to ,Daisy • said, " I have the , pleasure, I believe, of . speaking tc Miss Daisy Graham. ? "</p>
        <p>Daisy bowed. " The yourp, lady is Miss Graham," said her aunt in a Frigid voice, 'tor the ,cool, manner in which Mr:Rowlands had practically ignored her irritated the old .Spinstor to the '.verge of exasperation. "'She is my niece. I. am her guardian. Pray, sir, what may your business be with her ? "</p>
        <p>, "My business, madath," said Mr. Rowlands, turning upon the elderly lady as if to examine het more fully, " is so entirely private that I must request an interview with Miss Graham alone."</p>
        <p>" An interview with Miss Graham alone? " said Miss Princeps, rising from the couch and rustling her silk .dress as she rose, whilst Fido, shot :flom her lap, began to. make short rushes,l piinctuated with barks, at Mr. Row- lands. '• - • ' "At this hour of the night, alone ! Why, heaven bless me I " cried Miss Princeps, what's the world coining to ? What's the man thinking abut---what's--" • ."Wll you .kindly keep your dog to yourself, ;ma'am," said Mr. RoWlands, whose naturally brittle temper suddenly snapped, "or shall I be obliged to keep him off with my walltity,i stick ? " He. had entered the room hat and isticktinshand. " I ask for an interview with 'Miss ''Graham alone, for a .special business reason. It is iMmaterial, to me, utterly, the choice lies with • you, Miss Graham," again turning td Daisy, . " whether the intervVw is between us two or in .the presence of your aunt."</p>
        <p>"Oh," said Daisy, "in my aunt's presence, , please. I do not know why it should be otherwise. Who are you, sir ? "I am the manager of the London and Lancashire Bank," said Mr. Rowlands_ Daisy at the name turned deathly pale—" and my business with xou,•Miss Graham, is simply to ask you if you are the author of this letter received by one of my clerks." To Daisy's horror he drew from his pocket the letter she had written to Arthur Esmond, drew it out before the whole world, or, rather, what wa4 worse, before Miss Princeps. The girl's face flushed scarlet as she took her letter from the bank manager and glanced at it.</p>
        <p>"It is mine," she said. "Now," said Mr. Rowlands, "I want you to open that letter and read the paragraph I have underscored in red ink.'</p>
        <p>phe opened the letter and read the fatal paragraph. " Did you write that? 'I • Yes.'' " To what did you refer ? I No answer. "I do not wish to worry you," said Mr. Rowlands in a not unkind voice, "but I must ask you to answer this question absolutely truthfully. It is of enormous importance to</p>
        <p>yourself and to the person to whom this letter is addressed. I may say that very much depends upon your words." Daisy was appalled. She knew from the tone of the manager, from his face, from his hearing, that important issues hung upon her , answer ; she felt she must speak the truth, but to speak before her aunt I It would have been bad enough to have had to confess to that unsympathetic person her love for Arthur, and the fact that she had met him clandestinely. But it was infinitely worse to tell the tale before her aunt -and to a stranger that the paragraph demanded. That she had agreed to Arthur visiting her in the garden, he, a young man to whom she had never been introduced, was the plain, unvarnished state- ment she was called upon to make. kj Then suddenly the thing struck her in a new light. What right had Arthur to show her letter_ Could anything be meaner than such an act committed evidently to shield himself from some scrape about money matters? She had such a vague notion of the world and of money matters, and everything con- nected With business, that she could not grasp the idea that Arthur must be in a very serious position. She turned on Mr., Rowlands, flushed and haughty. "I utterly refuse to explain anything about this letter." "Is that your decision ? " "Yes." " I will give you time to reconsider it."' "Time," cried Daisy. "If you give me a hundred years my answer would be the same." "But think of the issue? " " I think of nothing. Row dare Mr. Es- mond show my letter I ' "You refuse to explain ? " "I refuse to continue this conversation." Miss Princeps, who had been sitting on the sofa open-mouthed, was absolutely too as- tonished to speak. Mr. Rowlands went, bowing coldly to both ladies as he left the. room. He was annoyed at his treatment yet he did not feel any resentment towards Daisy Graham. He knew that she must have suffered severe- ly, that the whole again was a brutal as- sault upon the finer part of her nature, and as he wended his 'way home he almost took sides with her against Arthur Esmond. He forgot that Arthur in giving up the letter had chosen between the least of two evils, that if he, Mr. Rowlands, had not read the letter the detectives a New Scotland Yard would have done so. He forgot that it was for the purpose of saving Miss Graham's reputation as well as his own that Esmond had told the true mean- ing of the paragraph. If he had only thought of these things he might have gone back to the house and explained matters ansi made every: thing straight between Daisy miff her lover. But he never thought of such a thing, and little cared, whether two lovers were separated perhaps for ever by a misunderstanding or not. Love and Mr. Rowlands, as we have already said, were things very far apart. ' Miss Princeps had sat without speaking during Daisy's onslaught upon the bank manager; she had never seen the hidden fire that lay in the Lind of her niece break out before. We find it often in life. We live for per- haps years witki a person without knowing their character or their possibilities, then a crisis comes in life and they reveal themselves for good or bad. " I could never have imagined," said Miss Princeps at last, standing erect and stiff and holding the back of a chair. " I could never have imagined this. Flightiness I suspected, untidiness, neglect of the ordinary ameniti.:-, of life--,--" "What do you mean by amenities of life ? " asked Daisy irritably. The girl was walking up and down the room feverishly after the fashion of a person who is wounded in spirit. "Don't snap the words from my mouth," cried her aunt, who had, it must be said, the most vague notion of the meaning of the term, having picked it up out of a book as a catch phrase. " I will not be cross ques- tioned. I say I never could have imagined</p>
        <p>"What do you mean by this? " " This. I mean assignations with young men, letters from young men. I mean deceit —I mean everything wicked, ' She cea3ed. Daisy had left the room.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII. • SHE sougnt her bedroom. The 'unfortunate girl felt that a crisis had come in her life. Life under its present conditions had be- come unbearable. Wounded in her pride, in her love, and in her faith, harassed by he:: aunt with no hope in the future of better things, she determined to make an end of ji all; she had often dreamed of it before, an, now she determined to go forth into the grea big world free, to leave the relative that mad 4 her life a misery, and cat herself into thl arms of, chance, heedleff of what migl happen, - The clocks were striking ten as she sa, down at the table in her bedrrom %to write letter to Arthur. She wrote three long letters and tore them up one after the other. Then she wrote this:</p>
        <p>"After What has happened to-day I 'will never see you again. Mr. Rowlands will ex- plain I</p>
        <p>"DAISY GRAHAM." This she put in an envelope which she duly addressed to " Arthur Esmond, Esq., Monta- gue Place, Russell Square, W.C.' , Having done this in a most business-like manner. and having gummgd the envelope, she broke into tears.</p>
        <p>They did not last long, however. The dawn was at the window. Six hours had passed like a moment in the writing of thf.,se' Letters., Time at critical periods of our lives has the power of Condensing itself like this. Daisy stamped the letter, placed it on the table, and then looked around9ter.</p>
        <p>Everyothing in her room, .e'xcept the furni- ture, was hers, llut everything that was hers had been bought with her aunt's money. •</p>
        <p>Her aunt had not been niggardly to her in the matter of dress and jeweller), , quite the re- verse. Miss Princeps was proud and required that her niece should be well dressed, lust a..; ishe required that her horses should be weil groomed and her maids miracles of neatness. She had dresses from Redfern .and Yaqui'', I jewellery from the Rue de la Paix ; she lia(i everything that a girl could possibly want, and she was going to leave it all behind. She felt that she could not take anything away with her except bare necessities, and for tho,x she would repay her aupt when she had tamed some means of making money. She wished to be behdlden for nothing to I the•woman who had never spoken a kind word to her except before strangers, who had fed her body and starved her soul. She went over all her clothes and chose a neat dress of blue serge which seemed to her the most serviceable and wear resisting of her ' many gowns; she went over her boots and shoes and chose a pair of the strongest. • Then she put a few things, in a small gladstone bag, choosing them with the care and considera- tion of a campaigner who wishes to be bur- thened with nothing useless. There was a sovereign and three shillings in Ler purse. She determined to take those also. Having chosen a fawn felt from her many hats and a stout umbrella, she sat down at the table ready dressed for walking, and wrote. the following on a. sheet of paper. " I have left your house for ever. Ido not know where lam going. God will guide me. I have taken my purse with I 38. 6d. in it and the clothes I wear. I will repay you for Wiese when I am able to earn some money. You have made my life a misery and I can idure it no longer. • • !` DAISY GRAHAM." pllced this in an envelope and directed it to Miss Priiceps. Itien, with the gladstone bag and umbrella m onc! hand and the two letters in the other, she came dowm,tlirs.</p>
        <p>A housemaid was cleaning the hall. " Give</p>
        <p>t ;c1 ie lie 11. t It a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER VII</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII</p>
        <p>Miss PRINCEPS was •an elderly lady who knew her own' mind; this did not imply A very great extent of knowledie, but it *as quite sufficient for Miss Princeps' needs J And it seemed that what Miss Princeps deeded most in the worid was someone to nag; failing a particular person, people in general served her purpose. She was "a terror to the servants "' • from the boot boy to the butler they held her in awe. She was always well served, for she was a bulry, and she was always respected for she was rich. ,</p>
        <p>She was thin and angular and wore side curls and spectacles. Her mind was the counterpart of her body, prim, angular, cor- rect, and ugly. Ugly, that is to say, in the sense of not being attractive, for, as her face held no decided' feature, her mind seemed destitute of any decided vice or virtue. . .</p>
        <p>Patience Flrinceps Patience was her Christian' name—was the' eldest daughter of James Princeps, head of the great firm oi Princeps and Princeps, wool brokers, of Liver- pooL James Princeps had only two children to inherit his wealth, girls both—Patience, as like himself as a daughter can be like a father, and Amelia, who, people said, took after her dead mother.</p>
        <p>Nothing cOuld be more unlike than these Vvo girls. • Patience, from the time when she had first begun to think, had made up her mind that the correction of Amelia was the purpose for which the Almighty had constructed her and sent her into the world. • Amelia thought differently. Amelia, • gOrden-haired and blue- eyed, had made up.-'her mind that tile world was a place meant to be lived in, not to lle bullied in.</p>
        <p>The result may be imagined: from their earliest day,s the sisters had quarrelled. Amelia, •sunny tempered but passionate, stood the corrections and nagging of her elder sister till, just as a Leyden jar fills with electricity, her mind would fill with resentment and then there would-be a storm.</p>
        <p>At the age of eighteen Amelia, who was then one of the prettiest girls in Birkenhead, ran away with Herbert Graham, a drawing master, well connedted but poor: The rage of old Princeps at this mesalliance cannot adequately be described with pen on paper. It is sufficient to say that he cut Amelia off with a shilling, left all his money to Patience, and died.</p>
        <p>Amelia had a baby and died. The child was christened Daisy. Herbert. Graham, who had always been a delicate man, never re- covered the blow given to him by his wife's death, a lingering consumption developed, and three years after her mother's death Daisy was left an orphan. Patience Princeps stepped in at this junc- ture and rescued her sister's child from the workhouse. All through the, five years of Amelia's married life; Patience had 'neither spoken nor written, to her sister, but the idea of her own niece being brought up by the parish was too much for the, pride of Miss Princeps, and she sent for the child, found her, as far as a baby can be like an adult, the image of her sister, obtained a nurse for her,, and formally took over, charge, so to ipeak, of the unfortu- nate inf Ant's body and soul. She had someone to correct at least, and it was from day to night an eternal round of "Daisy, don't do this," and "Daisy, don't do that," and "Daisy, why will you be such a wicked till the golden headed Daisy from incessant, iteration became possessed of a sense of moral obliquity such as few hoary headed sinners possess. She went to a school some half a mile distant from Miss Princeps' house in Boundary Road, a large boarding school where the girls went out for walks two by two, the great girls in front and the little girls behind, and as the years passed and , she grew in knowledge and grace no girl in the Whole of Miss Simperson's Academy could compare with, Daisy, in sweet- ness of mind or in beauty. At eighteen, she -was taken away, to use Miss mperson's expression, "finished," which means in ordinary parlance "just begun." She had finished her school education, it is true, but the education that life gives us all had not even begun. • Arthur Esmond gave her her ..rst lesson ; he was her first teacher in that great academy sailed the world.</p>
        <p>They met in quite a dramatic manner on the MetrOpolitan Railway when Arthur Es- mond had the keen and lively satisfaction of smacking a man's face who had. pushed against and insulted a tall beautiful girl with golden hair, who wore a picture hat and—to complete the photograph—was carrying 'a strap-full of books from Mudie's.</p>
        <p>They met again and again, and yet again; their intercourse was quite friendly at first and quite frank. He told her who he was, how his father had lost money on the Stock Exchange, and died leavin,, him, Arthur, an orphan. How he worked° in a bank and hoped some clay to be manager. She to,la Mm how she, too, was an orphan. She told him of Miss Princeps, and Miss Princeps played for them both the part of the ogre 'in their fairy tale. For it was a fairy tale in which, after the first few meetings they had h2d together they felt they had never lived before, simply be they had never loved before. Never .seemed flowers so blue or skies so bright as during the first , few weeks of this courtship, this dumb courtship, if one may • use the term, for at first they spoke nothing of love, that is to say, with their tongues. One day all this changed. _ .</p>
        <p>Miss Piinceps had begun to suspect some- thing, perhaps it was the new light of happi- ness in her niece's face which .tgold the dour old maid what had taken place in her niece's life ; perhaps it was simply suspicion begotten of the girl's beauty,; at any rate, the fiat had ,gone forth/that Daisy was, to take no more walks abroad unattended by a maid. , "And, oh ! " said Daisy, as she told her lover this doleful news, "you should see the maid she has chosen, sour and crabbed look- ing just like herself." "I shall go to your aunt and explain matters."</p>
        <p>"Don't," said Daisy,. "you don't know her as I do, she'll never listen to you, you are too poor, Pippy, dear, and you're in a bank. Oh, dear I wish there were no such things 'as banks or aunts, and no such thing as money."</p>
        <p>They then had a long consultation as to whatshould be done. No two old diploma- tists laying their heads together are as clever as tw6 lovers, and after their conference, which was conducted, by the way, in a tea room in the City, where Daisy met him after banking hours, the following plan of campaign was fixed on,</p>
        <p>Miss Princeps' house, " Th;,,j,aurels," ' was situated about the middle of Boundary Road, St. John's Wood. A lane ran by the garden wall, which was between seven and eight feet high ; and Arthur was to scale the wall and meet Daisy in the garden after dusk,</p>
        <p>It was a large garden, where they might walk and talk unobserved, sheltered by the shrubs and trees, There was a side door, but it was always kept locked; besides, had the key been available Arthur would not have used it—to sneak into a garden by a back door was an action that did not commend itself to his mind. To climb a wall at the risk of being caught was different somehow and savoured of romance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>'LIFELESS GIRLS</head>
        <p>'LIFELESS GIRLS</p>
        <p>Whose Blood ,is Poor and Watery, Wretched, with Sick Headaches, Weak Backa, and Fainting Fits, Made liVsll and Bright; Cured by</p>
        <p>VILWILLUS' PINK PILLS'</p>
        <p>The agonies which make the lives of many of our women-folk miserable, and lead to afflictions that end too frequently in decline are illustrated in the case of Miss Jennie Fleming, of 46, Kerr-street, Portrnsh, who withia the past few months has proved how valuable Dr. Williams', Pink Pills for Pale People are in -the ailments of her sex. . "I became alarmed at my low state of health," Miss Fleming stated, "being quite unable to take any interest in ordinary affairs. I felt lifeless and possessed of a strange dread of company. "A short walk or run up a few stairs fatigued me , severely, aid left me breathless with palpitation. I I had excruciating pains in my back and a constanegnaw- . ing pain in my legs. "At times I felt almost hysterical over my uncertain and irregular health, and 'I became very melancholy and irritable. As months passed ligrew worse rapidly. My eyes lost their brightness ; I %MIS pale, and each morn- ing I awoke with a headache, and felt too heavy to rise. I lost appetite, but had an unusual thirst, which only , tea would quench. Naturally I soon became a victim ' to indigestion. Frequently I was seized with giddiness leading up to fainting fits and sickness. I tried many medicines, but could not find a real cure until at last I was urged to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. "I hesitated, as girls generally do, to consult others, or doubtless I should have had this recommendation earlier. Now lam anxious to repeat that recommenda- tion. "I took Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for a few weeks, and very easily all my troubles were dispelled. I recovered strength, enjoyed regular health, and felt so much brighter in spirits that I was like another girl. My health was changed completely for the better, and for this blessing I never fail to thank Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People." For languishing, lifeless, ansairic girls and women there is no remedy equal to 'Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. They fill the veins with rich good blood that restores strength, stimulates the nerves and builds up the system. They have cured An&amp;inia, Indigestion, Eczema. Rheumatism, Sciatica, St. Vitus' Dance, Paralysis, Nervous Disorders. and the many ailments of the weaker sex. Sold at shops, but avoid substitutes, and see that the full name is on, every package, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People; or direct from the British Depot, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, post free, 2s. 9d. one box, or six for 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Hatton Horticultural Society.</head>
        <p>Hatton Horticultural Society.</p>
        <p>The -distribution of the prizes awarded at the recent horticultural show was made by the Rev. T. T. Lee Jones, president of the society. There was a good attendance of those interested in the society's welfare, including many members of the committee. The Secretary (Mr. G. Hatton) stated that the society had had a record attendance at this year's ethibition, and that the amount of money to be distributed in prizes was £45 Bs. in addition to special medals, &amp;c. (Applause.) The Vicar of Daresbury then distributed the prizes, after which the Hon. and Rev. C. F. Cross proposed that a hearty vote of thanks be accorded to Mr. Hatton, who did his utmost, not only on the show day but throughout, the year to advance its welfare. (Applause.) This was seconded by the Rev. T. T.Lee Jones and heartily carried. In reply Mr. Hatton thanked those present for their kind appreciation of his services, and begged to acknowledge the valuable assistance he invariably received from the members of the committee. Mr. Thomas Warburton then pro- posed a vote of thanks to the President for is services. This was seconded by Mr. R. L. Warburton and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL STATISTICS.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL STATISTICS.</p>
        <p>The returns of Congregationalism throughout the world have just been collected. The following figures show the,number of churches, and mission churches of the Congregational order :—Great Britain 4,905, Canada land Newfoundland 147, British Guiana and Jamaica 96, Australia and New Zealand 379, South Afric.a 360, Asia 128, United States of America (which include the Istations of the American Board of Congregational Forei,ge Missions) 9,061. This gives a total of 15,076' churches, chapels, and stations. The total membership I represented by these churches and stations is given as 11,300,426, with 1,596,620 scholars in Sunday schools. These figures do not include stations and membership of London Missionary Society, which is recognised as a Congregational institution and which in its last published report showed a total of 80,165 members, 240,890 adherents, and 55,852 Sunday school scholars.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " gemp's Mercantile. Gazette," the total number of Commercial failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, August 17th, was 148, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 76; Deeds of Arrangement registered,72. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 84 ; Deeds of Arrangement 70—total 154; being a decrease of 6. The total num- ber of Commercid Failures recorded during the 33 weeks of the present year was 5,365 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 33 weeks of last year was 5,736, showing a decrease of 371.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>er work</head>
        <p>er work</p>
        <p>Work, on wash-day and every other day, is easier if you use Fels-Naptha soap. Use it "the Fels-Naptha way," remember. °</p>
        <p>"The Fels-Naptha way" of washing is to moisten the clothes; rub on Fels-Naptha; roll up each article; soak for half-an-hour; rub a little; rinse thoroughly in two or three clean waters; wring out and dry.</p>
        <p>Use cold water—or warm for comfort's sake. Don't use hot water and don't boil tl_e clothes.</p>
        <p>Get your money back from the dealer if you don't think "the Fels-Naptha way" the best. ,</p>
