      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Smoot FOR HorsEwaxnY.</head>
        <p>A Smoot FOR HorsEwaxnY.</p>
        <p>IA real ,boon to the country, says Mrs. Charles .Marshall, would be a national school of house: wifcry, but it would be impossible for such a ;place to be self-supporting, and no private enter- arise could afford to ?ivo time and capital for ar 15bject which obviously would be an expensive ,undertaking. If woi.len wero given the oppor- h,inity of making a special study 'under corn- pctent teachers of household manalement, and I.were armed at the finish with certificates of pro- ificiency. it would tend to raise the tone of I,domestio occupations considerably, and if care wore exercised' in the selection of candidates a I Eeld would at once be opened for clever, enter- prising girls where the demand far workers would be constant and the wages worth accepting I'o-DAY'S AECIPE.</p>
        <p>- ) CURRIED PRAWNS AND BPINACR. -- Tele Eb. I a gPinach, pick and wash it, and cook until I tender. Then drain and pros; out all the water ; and rub through a fine Bit`VO. Place I.oz. of 1 ' butter in a saucepan, and when hot stir in a 3.-ssertspoonful of finely-mixed onions, a tie-pearl- erx)onful of flour; and a level dessertspoonful o' the best curry powder. V.llien theee are ‘,;, Imixed add the sninach, and by dem:poen a coun;•, . at deasertspoontuis of thick cream, allowing aim !to simmer gently for fifteen minutes. ' If too ' thick add a little good gravy, and then stir ir. half a cupful of picked prawn&amp; Serve with a border of boiled me.</p>
