        <head>L ,„. ~.„.,. ~,.giii 1 i ,; 1111;tA7v 1111 tn. .3* STARCH r</head>
        <p>L ,„. ~.„.,. ~,.giii 1 i ,; 1111;tA7v 1111 tn. .3* STARCH r</p>
        <p>this note to my aunt, Maria," said Daisy, handing the girl one of the letters: " I am going out for a walk." " Yes, miss," answered the gift "Shall I give it to her at once, miss ? " "Oh, no," said Daisy, quite nonchalantly; "it will do when she comes down to breakfast. I shan't be in to breakfast. I shall be out for the day." Then she opened the door and passed out into the bright morning. At a pillar box close by she posted the letter to Arthur. If fell with a fateful sound into the jempty box. Then she passed down Boundary Road, the morning sun ;full upon her, and turned the corner. In London to be lost for ever you have only to turn a corner—you are lost as completely to individual view as a drop of water that falls into the sea. You are there, but you are gone to he who looks for you. It was seven o'clock when Daisy turned the "corner of Boundary Road; servants were shak- ing mats at doors, servants were whitening steps, polishing knockers and door handles ; the air fesbunded with the voice of the early- morning milkman. The baker's hand cart stood by the kerbstone, and the newspaper boy with his sheaf of newspapers darted from house to house with the rapidity Of a minnow In a stream ; there was that brightness and appearance of " newness " over everything which one only perceives in the early morn- ing when the sun is shining ; ibirds were sing- ing in the garctens, and the wind from, the west carried with it a faint odour reminiscent of green -fields and hedges, .rose-garderts, and ripening corn. Daisy as she walked almost forgot Miss •Princeps, Arthur, Mr. Rowlands, all the actors, in' fact, in her little tragedy ; so filled was she with the utter newness of her position. It was just as if a passenger on an ocean liner losing his balance had found himself in the Anttlaic Ocean. ' In an hour's time the breakfast gong would' sound, but she would not sit down to break- fast ; the luncheon ,time would come round, but she would not be there ; she would nol sleep in her room that night, nor give Fido hi- lump of sugar before she went to bed. B• 2 she would not sleep in her bed again.. old life was gone, no more assistance from anyone, not a friend, everything to get for her- self—and not an idea of how to set about getting it. But she was not in the least dispirited, quite the reverse. The newness of the situation lit by the brightness of the summer's morning. braced her, and she walked scarcely seeming to feel the weight of the bag she carried.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
