      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CUTS AND BRUISES. First-Aid For All the Family in a Handy Box of Zam-Buk.</head>
        <p>CUTS AND BRUISES. First-Aid For All the Family in a Handy Box of Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Accidents wilt happen even in the best regulated families. Healthy youngsters seem to be "always in the wars" ; cooking and cleaning abound with mishaps for busy housewives and harrassed mothers; and "father" often returns from work with a more or less serious injury. Just what every home needs.is Zam-Buk. A box and a bandage on a handy shelf, where they are ready for instant Ilse, save untold pain, time, and money. The little ones like Zam-Buk because it is so cooling, soothing, and pleasant. Parents like it because it is also antiseptic, preventing poisoning or festering ; because it is a natural skin-repairer, growing new tissue in Nature's way; and because it is good for the tender- est skin, being made of the pure skin medicines extracted from rich herbs. Miss Watson, of Yew Tree Farm Barbon, Middleton, near Kirby Lonsdale, writes :—" I, have suffertd dread- fully from cuts, bruises and burns,bnt have found Zara- Bak to be a great healer. I was one day chopping sticks and cut my finger very badly. I applied Zam-Buk at once, and the place is now quite healed. I also burnt myself very much while baking, and another day I bruised myself very badly while doing my housework. I Again Zam-Buk was quite successful. I now use the ' balm regularly, my practice being to wash wounds in water and then dress with Zam-Buk spread on a piece of clean lint. Nothing I have tried before for injuries has been anything like equal to Zam-Buk."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RAILWAY NERVE.</head>
        <p>THE RAILWAY NERVE.</p>
        <p>• The perils of the railway track are like the details of an auctioneer's advertisement—" too numerous to be • particularised." But there are just as many precautions on all big systems to safeguard the lives of their . passengers, and the engine-drivers' share in this I organized war against accident is breezily put in sn interview which Mr. Keighley Snowden contributes, with many fine photographs, to the September "Pall Mall Magazine." His interlocutor is a famous driver on one of the great lines from the Metropolis to the North, a man of hard experience and clear views, of prompt action and iron nerve. This, with some allow- ance for his Yorkshire accent, is the gift of the talk : "There's no man keen to be out on foggy nights, or. ~ when at snows much—specially if he's a fast train to run. A driver should know whereabouts be is, you see. At every speed he has to judge where he can stop her ; but, when you cannot see what place it is you're passin', I that's a job. . . . You're lookin' out and hearkenin' ; I you think,' I'm close again' that signal somewhere'— and yet you run for long afore you find it. I' "But snowstorms .e they're a masterpiete. They don't. come so suddenly on as fog can do, but then they'll block up signal-lights. *ipty's been known to pull a signal off ! Ay, or a nice rimey frost'll bother you ; times when a man can't see nowgh't through his peep- holes, and has to put his head outside—twenty yards 'll freeze your moustache. It will. Frost and ashes ! Put youriace to the fire and wipe it—out again! I tell you it's a twister." "A driver's main enemy is wind ; and that'll surprise you. Not a head-wind so much, but a side-wind. Do what we will, sometimes, we cannot keep up speed becepse of a wind blowine. It's friction, wheels thrust against the metals, and its wind resistance. Wind's like water when it blows hard. There's bulk and weight in it. Now, to-day's been plain sailin', calm weatheran' a grand sweet run. Well, we've burned ten or fifteen hunderdweight o' coal less in a hunderd mile nor what we shall in wind. .You look; but lean tell of a stormy night—this last March, it wor—wnen more by twenty hunderdweight Wore burned. Two tubs. A ton o' coal. Put that on two hours' work fora fireman." "Mark you, she's a willin' engine. Just let her know what there is to be done, and keep her in heart, she'll do it. A good engine makes a good driver. I know what mine can do as well as a jockey knows his horse. Ay. and an engine's like a horse : she'll tell you herself if aught be wrong.. A box gets warm, we'll say ; well, she'll soon let you smell it. It comes on the wind, puffin', an' then you know to stop her. Drop a hit of paper out with a -word to the signalman. But, if she sulks, and won't drive, that's domino. That means a leaking tube. There nowght to do but coax her then ; and how we reach our journey's end we never in this world know," The slackened bolt or the heated bearing may he a fault of the driver's own. So may " priming," that mischief of impaired efficiency which is the subtle sign of injudicious watering. To avoid it, he must be exact with his injector, keeping a nice equivalent of water and of coal. Time and the signals govern this, a third preoccupation ; and they govern it so continuously. with give-and-take in a delicate quick adjustment, that a good mate's eyes are never off the driver. "And don't imagine," said my friend, "that when we've done a journey all's concluded. You'd be far wrong. A driver's day begins % in the shed and ends there. He books on; his engine's stood and slept, Wl' fiitters overhauling her, and cleaners • but when he comes to her he looks at every bolt. Good shed-work means good runnin '." Isn't it wonderful ? The signal-lights are sometimes quite a galaxy ; and as the line swings round ita curves they seem to change their places. How, from every given point, does a driver know his own? "That's his job. That's what he learns at firing." Fitter, driver, master mariner. Upon reflection, the fact may be that twenty or thirty years is not too long a probationership for this rare skill. _ _</p>
      </div>
