      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 25, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 25, 1906.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.</p>
        <p>IJack. Not one of the old haunts but I looked right through. Who would have thought of you living here in Claridge-street, and under such a swell name, too? Abingdon! Mr. Abingdon: Why, ain't you quite, a toff ? " "Is this some acquaintance, Ralph ? " asked While the stranger had been speaking, she stood helpless, watching her husband's face. 1 Where was the courteous, the brilliant, • the versatile Ralph Abingdon? Where was thc chiva'rmr;, polished gentleman, 'the soul of honour, the devoted husband? A new look was on his face, an expression Emily had never seen there before. "I Say,' Tootal," said Abingdon, "come round to-morrow and see Inc." _ .</p>
        <p>"Oh!" sneered the wretch, "got. compsnY, Jack? Is she front the Gaiety or from .the Alhambra F" .</p>
        <p>"I am 'Mr. Abingdon's wife,. sir," said Emily, rising from her seat. •., • For the next half-minute Tooial's face wes study. He had not troubled to remove his-hat; it was perched on his head in a rakish inkmaci. his feet were stuck wide apart, and there had been a clay pipe in his mouth when he metered the room. When Emily rose from her seat and hurled the unexpected news at him, the bit of clay dropped from the rascal's lips, and Tootar mouth screwed itself into a whistle. ."What man is this?" asked Emily of her</p>
        <p>husband. "Never heard of me, my dear?" said Tootal. "Sorry I ain't got my card-case. Uncle's sporting it just now. )3less me, Jack, you've done a good thing. Ain't you win' to perform the sarimony of a perlite hintrodookshun "? " "Mr Tootal is an old friend of mine, Emily." • "Wants your husband on pressing busineszt my dear. Yes, Jack, come along." "My husband's name is not Jack, sir." "Oh! ain't it? And what is he calling him- self just now ? " "I have always known him as Ralph Abingdon," said Emily, fixing her eyes suddenly upon the gentleman referred to. , Again Tootal screwed his mouth into, a—whistle —his very eyebrows seemed to whistle—and tipped his hat a little further on to the back of his head. Then he almost doubled. up, 118 a big gurgle, beginning somewhere in his boots, gradually developed inta a side-splitting laugh. "Ralph!" ho exclaimed. "Air. Ralph nr)ing- don ! That's a good joke!" "Shall. I leave the &amp;elm, dear ? " asked Emily, who saw that her husband was somewhat puL about.. s "Never!" 'cried Tootal, "She'll stay. with us. Jack. -Come and kiss me, dear. Why, ain't she gots, face!" The rogue made a steil.forward, but a• glance from Emily stopped him.' "Come away out. Jack, there's a job for us," hp said, shuffling towards the door. 'You are not' going with this man?" cried Emily, rushing to her, husband and placing her hand on his arm.</p>
        <p>Tootal stood in: the doorway. The merry roguery whieli had been, playing on hia faeo changed to an expression of envy and brutal force.</p>
        <p>"Jack, ole pal," he said, come?"</p>
        <p>"I guess you'll</p>
        <p>"No," whispered Emily. "I must." And the two men left the house. Poor Emily was in despair. She could not understand her husband. The sudden appearance of this stranger had altogether transformed him. She had never known much of his past, and now a great fear haunted her. Who was this rough intruder? Whence had he come? Whither was he taking her husband? Obeyirvg a sudden impulse, Mrs. Abingdon put ,on her hat and followed them. On and on they walked, usually :down the by-streets. At length she saw them vanish up a dark close in Long Acre. Emily •came up to the entrance. The place was dark and squalid. A filthy odour was made even more filthy by the London fog. A slatternly woman came out.and gazed at the strange lady. Her feet were bare; so were her arms up to the elbows. She held a squalling child in her hands; the meteor herself was reeking with gin. Emily pulled dot her purse and put a sixpence in the baby's hand. ' The wonmn grabbed it, slapped the child, and ' hastened to the nearest bar. For a momeM. Emily hesitated. Should she give up her quest? Should she go on?. A Higher Hand seemed to , be leading her. Once more obeying impulse, she ascended the creaky stair. Up to the first flat, up to the second; on the third.-,floor she heard Tootal's voice. It was raised in anger. Emily paused at the door of a front room. Was her husband there? Was he the object of Tootal's wimp? What could a woman do? "It's false!" cried Tootal, furiously. "Turn over a new leaf ? All rot! It's this girl who's come round you, Jsek, and you've got to drop her. Think we're going to lose a pal like you Not by a big jugful I" "I tell you, Tootal, it 'can't be done. She's my lawful wife, and a jolly good one too. I'2'e been a better man since I knew her, and the old set shan't know me any more." • "The oath!" cried Tootal. "What was the oath, mate? Nothing but death should part us. We've been in too many things together, Jack, my boy. S'pose you'd like to peach ? " "You know I'm not that sort," said Abingdon. "I'll hold you to 'the oath," cried Tootal. "Nothing but death shall part-us. We'll leave this room as pals, or you never leave it again, Jack." The door was closed with a slam. Tootal had thrown off his coat and drawn a long knife from his belt. He placed his back to the,panel. • "Now," he shouted, "which is it to be, mate— pals or the knife?" Emily heard the words. Pushing with all her might, she forced the door•open and confronted the two men. "Ralph," she entreated, "I am true to you. You will never go back. Have nothing to do with this man." "Oh, oh!" roared Tootal, and before Abingdon could interfere he had felled the young wife to the ground, closed the door, and once more con- fronted his confederate. There was a brutal ex- pression on his face. "Pals, or the knife!" he cried again: Not another word was spoken. Emily lay senseless on the floor. Both men stripped to the waist, and the conflict began. Fire burst forth %from the fiat below; they noticed it not. A crowd collected in the street; their own room became full of .smoke. They noticed nothing. Their eyes were bloodshot; their arms were streaked with blood. "Pals, or the knife!" Tootal was fighting in a frenzy. Ralpil Abingdon, with Emily stretched there on the 'floor, fought for honour and freedom. "Stop!" With one stroke of his fireman's axe, Old Wales dashed the knife out of Tootal's hand. "See to her, Frank," he cried, pointing to Emily. But the command was needless. Already the young greman had lifted' the unconscious form and borne it to the window. "What is the meaning of this, Abingdon?" demanded Old Wales of his son-in-law. "I can't explain." "But you've got to explain. My child has been placed in clanger: You might have been all lama alive. Out by that window, quick! Take Emily home. I'll be round in the evening, and I reckon you'd better make a clean breast of it." Mindful of his duty above everything, the hoes assisted T cltal and Ralph Abingdon to make their escape. It was no easy thing. The flames seemed ravenous: Up and up they soared; the whole building was one mass of fire. A cheer from the crowd , apprised Old Wales that his daughter had reached the 'ground in safety.</p>
        <p>_ "Now!" he exclaimed. - Tootal went first; Abingdon followed. The fireman was the last to leave the building. . ,</p>
        <p>The property leas doomed. The firemen eonld do nothing, and Frank Goodall himself took Emily to her house. The fresh air. had revived her, and by the time they reached Claridge- street Mrs. Abingdon was herfelf again. But not a word did she speak; she offered no explana- tion of how she and her husband came to be in the doomed building. . "Something wrong," said Frank to himself, as he resigned her to the maid. "Who was that second chap? Can't make it out. Halloal Here he comes!" For even as Goodall stood on the steps, Tootal approached the door. "HADA!" said FAnk. "What do you want hf.re " "What 1 like. Let me in." For the young fireman had gripped the mace; by the arm. "You move off," he said, firmly. "No, I won't." "Won't you ? " "NO."</p>
        <p>Frank pulled out his fireman's whistle. "gove off at once," he said, "or I call the police." Police! Thu word acted like magic on the rogue; Scowling fiercely, and threatening vengeance, Tootal nevertheless slouched away. Frank followed him to the corner' ' and saw him pass down the next street. Then Frank turned hiss eyes back to 4, Claridge-street. All that he loved on earth was there, although she was =other's. "It's no business of mine," he thought, "they're husband and wife now. Eat she needs a friend, I'm sure of it. That fellow may come back. I'll keep an eye on the house." Tootal -did come back; he came back that evening. Ralph Abingdon was lying on a couch, his arm in a sling. Paler than usual, Emily sat in a chair beside him.</p>
        <p>"Hah !" laughed Tootal, as he strode into the room. "Here we are again, Jack, and the girl too."</p>
        <p>"Leave this house, sir," said Mrs. Abingdon. "My husband is hurt. He nearly lost his life through you this morning. Leave this house at once."</p>
        <p>The only reply which Tootal made was to take off his hat and coat and deposit them on the table • - "I'm here to stay," he said, coolly. "You'll not turn a fellow out, Jack? The Long Acre den is done for, and the ',Q,cipA' are running me hard. I guess I'm safe the house of Mr. Ralph Abingdon." The rogue smjled sardonically as he pro- nounced the name. His tongue lingered upon it as on a tit-bit. Emily threw an entreating glance towards her husband.</p>
        <p>," 'Fraid it's impossible, Tootal,'! said Abingdon. He was womided ; he dared not quarrel with his confederate. "What, man! I thought we'd 'settled i. I could have stuck you like a pig to-day. Come, let's be pals." Tootal was exaggerating. Ralph Abingdon had held his own in the sanguinary conflict. He was not going to yield now. "No," he said, rising, "it's all up between us, Bob. I have chosen my way, and if you don't like it you've got to go your own. I'm not the man to peach, but I've got a wife now. I won't be forced away from her." "Forced! This will force you." A magnificent candelabra stood on the table. Turelve candles burnt in it. Grasping the silver stand in his left hand, Tootal advanced to the rich silk curtains which hung at the window. "Now, Jack,".he cried, "you know me and I know you. This woman has come between us. You've got to give her up. Shake hands on it, or I fire the house."</p>
        <p>"No you don't!" cried, a voice. 'There was a crash, the window was shivered to pieces, and Frank Goodall leaped into die room. "Villain ! " he roared. "1 find you here again." Tootal staggered back from the window. For a moment he felt cowed; then, as he glanced from the two men to the woman trembling in the background, he divined how matters stood, and saw his chance of a double vengeance. "Look here, Jack," he said to Emily's husband, "this fellow loves your wife." , "Nonsense!" exclaimed Ralph Abingdon. "Then what does he mean by standing 'outside her window ? "</p>
        <p>All eyes were turned on the young fireman. Frank Goodall looked to the floor. He was not a man of quick device. Honest and true, his strength ley in action. Could he speak the words that rose to his lips? No. Emily had chosen another. He must be silent.</p>
        <p>Tootal was mas,ter of the situation. "So!" he sneered, advancing to the table. "Two men and one woman, eh? A pretty state of,things, Mrs. Ralph Abingdon." "Dog !," exclaimed her husband. Tootal fret ,down the candelabra slowly, and drew a revolver from his pocket. "It strikes me," he said, coolly, "there's only one way out of this mess, Jack. You both love the girl—which of you is going to kill himself ?" ' Emily clutched her husband's arm. "Come, Mr. Fireman," sneered the rogue. "Are you man enough to pull the trigger for her sake ? " _</p>
        <p>Frank Goodall raised his eyes. "Yes," he ! answered, advancing to the table. "Stop!" shouted Abingdon, rushing forward. "You shall not do this, Goodall. It is I who have brought this stain upou her life. But I loved her—yes, I loved her, for she saved me from the past." "And she will save you now!" It was Emily who spoke. Sweeping across the room, she confronted the three men with blazing eyes. All weakness was past. Her woman's heart was roused.</p>
        <p>"Are you mad, both of you?" she cried. "Frank Goodall, we have known each other since childhood. Have you lost, your senses? Open that door, and hand this rascal to the police! And Ralph husband Great Heaven!"</p>
        <p>For there was a report, a sudden cry, and Abingdon fell to the floor. Tootal had shot him through the heart. "Villain!" shouted Frank Goodall: springing across the table. Too late The murderer had seized the candelabra and set the silk curtains on fire. In a moment the room was in flames. N Tootal b spranr, to the window, intending to leap into the street. The wretch gave a shriek as Frank pinned him by the neck, and dragged him back into the burning room. The place was full of smoke, the flames burst through the ceiling, and penetrated to the flat above. The two men struggled together, their clothing was torn, chairs and tables were overturned. There was a loud crash, as they fell in one heap upon the floor Emily was kneeling by her husbend. "Ralph!" she shrieked. "Oh, Ralph, have tlry killed you ? " A crowd had gathered in the street An alarm was raised, the Charing Cross Division of the Fire Brigade came dashing up. Followed by 4welve stout comrades the boss pushed open the dining-room door. The room was full of smoke; he coyld see nothing. "Here!" cried a faint voice from the window.</p>
        <p>The rescuers dashed forward, ,but even as they did so 014 Wales stumbled across the body of Emily's husband. Lifting him in his arms, he bore him out, followed by his daughter. "Here, lass he cried, laying him down. "Here he is, Emily." • "Ralph!" she cried, kneeling. beside him. Her tears fell on his face. But a doctor pushed through the crowd. "Stand back," he said, "let me see him." "Oh, he's all right," said the boss, not knowing that his son-in-law was dead. "They're husband and wife." "Ah!" said the doctor.</p>
        <p>His practised eye had told him the trnth in an instant. Tearing open the dead man's shirt, he found the place through which the bullet had passed. "What!" exclaimed Old Wales, suddenly. Bending down, he placed his finger upon a mark on Abingdon's breast. "Why, if this ain't that boy, Jack Broadhurst! Jack, lad?" But he spoke to deaf ears. a "I'm afi aid he's dead," said the doctor. "Dead ? shrieked Emily, as she fell upon the bay of the man she loved.</p>
        <p>• The crowd raised their Mts. aad?" said Frank Goodall, coming to her -Tootat had been handed over to the police. '"No," said Old Wales, brushing a tear from his stern eye. "Surely.not ?" "Yea," said the doctor, "life is extinct." , "Well, lass,". said Emily's. father after t pause, "you'd best come home with me, I'm thinking. 1-dur -own place is on fire, • your husband dead. You'd best come home with the phi Dad. The house has never been the smile without you."</p>
        <p>[THE ENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ORIGINAL COLOUR OF HAIR.</head>
        <p>THE ORIGINAL COLOUR OF HAIR.</p>
        <p>Red is believed by Dr. Beddoe to have been the original colour of the hair of Europeans, and he attributes the brown pigment to. the action .of heat. Red hair is occasionally found . among the negroes, and dull Thirk hair among the pigmies of Central Africa. Chinese and Japanese .adults al- ways have black hair, but Japanese children. sometimes have dark reddish ,Vellow hair, and Chinese children may have brown hair.</p>
        <p>IJAPANESE TELEGRAPH WITH FIGURES.• As the JaPanese have no alphabet, every word in their written language being represented by its own character, they have contrived a unique system for use in telegraphy. The Government bas compiled a dictionary Of 10,000 words com- monly used, and to each word is allotted a figure from one, Up to 9,999: The telegraph operator , assigns the proper figure to each word of a mes- sage to be sent, and then transmits the figures by i the Morse code. At the other end of tie line the message is translated by the aid of one of tho i Government dictionaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>. LIME DUST FOR CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>. LIME DUST FOR CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>Consut;nption and its cure have been engros- sing topics of conversation in Paris medical circles. At a meeting of the Academy of 'Medi- cine Dr. Drouardel held forth on the benefits to be derived by consumptives from living in an atmosphere charged with lime dust. He quoted' a report by Dr. Bordena,ye,. who. says that within 'phe thirty: years that he has practised. he has not teen a single case of consumption among the workmen at the kilns,. and that people af- fected with consumption are soon cured 'after they live some time in the lime dust.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WONDERFUL LITTLE ENGINE.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL LITTLE ENGINE.</p>
        <p>A watchmaker of Ontario has made a remark- able Working model of an engine. It weighs only four grains, and is made of gold and steel. It would take 2.20 of these almost microscopic en- Kirks to weigh an ounce, while 1,q20 would be re- pitied to weigh a pound, and 3.840,000 to weigh 3, top. The speed and horse-power of this little model .have been estimated by Professor Chant, f the University of Toronto. It makes 6,000 re- volutions Per miraute, and its working power is 1-453,000 th part of' a horse-power. The bore of its cylinder is 3-100ths of an inch in diameter, and the length of its stroke 1-32nd of an inch.</p>
        <p>SENSE. OF SMELL IN BIRDS. The question whether birds possess the sense of I smell or not has been much discussed. A study of the structure of the skull of the Apteryx, or !Kiwi, of New Zealand, suggests that this bird imust possess it in a high degree. The olfactory ! lobes of the brain are large, as also is the olfac- tory capsule. In Nature Professor W. 13. Ben- ham, of Dunedin, New Zealand, gives an intei, ekting account of some. experimental investiga- tions as to the sense of smell in this curious bird. Those were carried out on Resolution Island, which has been reserved as a sanctuary for birds by the curator, Mr. R. James. The species ex- perimented on is Apteryx australis, sometimes known as the roa-roa, and its chief food is earth. i worms. A number of earthworms were placed in a pail, and covered with four inches of earth. When presented to the bird ,it seemed to be aware that the .worms, were there, and immedi- ately probed to the . bottom for them. On the other hand, when a bucket with soil and no worm was offered, it would not look at it. The i suggestion that it may have heard the worms moving is negatived by the fact that when dead worms, which had been crushed with the spade in digging, were offered under like conditions they were probed for in the same way. It is to be remembered also that ,the bird malies a "sniffling noise" when feeding which would pre- vent it hearing any noise made by the worms. And it is to be noted in -connection with these experiments that according to some observers the bird is practically blind during the day, while the -tuft of hair-like feathers at the base of the beak would- hang between the eybs and• ground while it was feeding. Professor Benham con- cludes that the c.Apteryx has a "keen sense of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>KITES TO FLY UNDER WATER.</head>
        <p>KITES TO FLY UNDER WATER.</p>
        <p>The flying of kites was for many years to. garded as boys' sport, but those toys, as men were wont to regard them, have. become in the present day most valuable aids to Science. The strangest of all kites yet invented, says Soience 'Siftings, is one constructed by the famous Swedish engineer. Sjostrand. This kite is not to be flown in the air. It is to fly beneath the waters of the sea. It is made of light canvas," ad- justed to a light but strong metal .frarne, and in !shape is not dissimilar to the aerial kite, except ! that it is made in two sections. the lower and smaller one depending f4ora the upper, with which it is connected by a sort of coupling. The object sought by .Mr. Sjostrand was to provide . ships with an ever • ready automatic guard or watch that would give instant alarm if the vessel ; enters shoal waters, and is I approaching a spot where the depth is not sufficient for safety. The 'under water kite is fa.Stened to thin wire cable „ attached to a winch on deck. When the kite is I thrown overboard it sinks in consequence of the !oblique (slanting) pressure brought to bear upon lit by the water, . just as the land kite is raised and maidtairied aloft by the pressure of the air. With the winch the kite is lowered, or raised at will, and on a portion Of this winch i a scale and register that tells the depth in fathoms or meters at which it is floating. • Tbe purpose of the water-kite is to float be. neath or beside the ship at a depth that is stiffi• ' I ;ent to ensure safety. If, at any time, day Cr c' !night, the lower section of the kite strikes het. tont, a device instantly releases the coupling ; with which it is secured to the upeer section, and an alarm bell, is sounded on the deck of the I endangered vessel. Precautions can at once be ; taken to secure its safety.. The kite meanwhile is drawn up. readjusted, and thrown overboard ' to maintain its faithful watch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PURE OZONE.</head>
        <p>PURE OZONE.</p>
        <p>- The word ozone is associated in the public mind with the invigorating, influences of sea air. Whether or not this theOry is well founded, writes the lion. R. J. Strutt, F.R.S., it is diffi- cult to say. If it is, one would suppose that all the advantages of the sea air, and more besides, might be. got indoors even in towns by the artificial addition of ozone to the air of dwell- ing-houses; ozone, it will be remembered, is what is called an allotropic modification of oxygen. That is to say, it consists of the same ek,mentary material a.s oxygen, but in a more complex state of aggregation. Dr. Ladenburg, of Charlottenburg, has shewn that our knowledge on this subject was much less-complete than was for many years believed to be the case." Ozone is produced by the pas- sage of an electric discharge through oxygen. It is, however, impracticable to convert more than sinall proportion of the oxygen in this way. In order to concentrate the won° further, it is necessary to cool- the ozonico.l oxygen with liquid air, which as now becoming an ind.ispensable agent in the laboratory. Ozone •</p>
        <p>• CONDENSES TO A LIQUID • much more easily than oxygen, and thus can 'ha separated, from it by liquefaction.. The retsult- Mg liquid 'ozone has •a . deep blue colour and when allowed to boil Off to 'a gas still shews a bins Colour, not less conspicuous than the green of chlorine gas, which is familiar to. all chemical Pnre.ozone may be compared with chlorine in c,nother way: it .most injurious to the human lungs. Whether or not highly diluted ozone has healtliLgivinix .pro&amp;rties. it is very, Certain, that 110 snch properties belong to, the pure product. Dr. Ladenburg has; we believe, been more than once compelled to take to Ina' bed after getting a slight whiff of it. Pure ozone has another</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DISAGREEALL PECZLIARITY</head>
        <p>DISAGREEALL PECZLIARITY</p>
        <p>• in being explosive. It is , only concentrated ozone that behaves thus. We may compare its be- havieur with that of acetylene, which is quite safe at ordinary pressures, but becomes a clan- gorous explosive when compressed. The special interest of Dr. Ladenburg's work is this: he has been enabled to shew, by frac. tionally distilling liquid ozone, that it contains more than one Constituent. The greater part. consists of the ozone of chemical text-books, which consists of molecules, each built up of three oxygen atoms. But</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BESIDES THIS,</head>
        <p>BESIDES THIS,</p>
        <p>r nother still more .condenscd product is present, Its density is higher, and, in all probability, it consiett of aggregEtte of six atoms of oxygen. The absorption spectrum of crude liquid ozone. his several well-defined bands; that is to sav, there are certain definite wavelengths of light which cannot pa Isa through it. tis found that tlmott'of these bands belong to ozone proper, some of them to the new, ,morp Coadenzad product.</p>
        <p>, • Professor Dewar stat 4; that the new ehenustry I.las Ehewn beyond question that we have on earth the same elements that are feund in the sun.</p>
        <p>Electric waves follow he earth's curvature, explains Erskine-Murray, because a conducting layer, high in air, keeps them from spreading into space, practically causing them to travel he. tween two parallel conducting planes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>JACK BROADHURST'S RETURN.</head>
        <p>JACK BROADHURST'S RETURN.</p>
        <p>By GEORGE EENRY MANSFIELD, Author of "Amy Redfern's Ordeal," "Rebecca "Lost Souls," "Red Star," "In One Time," &amp;O.</p>
        <p>"I-tell you, Wales, no good can come of it." "We'll see, Frank:" . "We'll see some harm, Wales." "Well, well, Goodall, in love or, war the beat man wins." "I'm hanged if Abingdon is best man in this ease," said Frank Goodall. "I..esstweyS," said Harry Wales, "Emily thinks so." The speaker, wlio was boss of the Charing Cross Division of the Metropolitan' Fire firigade, was a big, broad, burly man, with square shoulders, a broad forehead, keen, grey-green eyes of pene- trating power. a head of brown hair turning grey, over the temples, and a stern, impassive expres- sion. Yet as he spoke there was a wistful look in his right eye and a soft touch abotit his mouth which seemed to hint that he felt somewhat uncertain about the matter in question. The two men confronted each other in the fire station. Goodall was one of the latest recruits, to the brigade—a youth of twenty-five, well-built, and with the spirit of a lion. He had rapidly became a favourite with Old Harry and all his comrades 'in the division, but though popular with them he would have given all their favour for one kind glance from Emily, the boss's onty daughter, a young girl of nineteen. Alas, however, for Goodall! Two months ago Emily had dashed all his hopes by rushing into a hasty marriage with Ralph Abingdon, a brilliant man of conspicuous talent, who had suddenly appeared on the scene and carried everything before him. "I tell you, Frank," said 110Ild Wales, "I have a kind of queer feeling in me here about this job. Emily and Abingdon have taken wonderfully to each other, and it kind of reminds me of the way she used to play when she was a lass, with that wild scamp, Jack Broadhurst, whop I' adopted. I have often told you the story. His father was a niate of 'mine when I first joined the brigade. At.the big fire in Tooley-street poor Broadhurst lost his life. We were up in the fourth storey of a grain ,Warehouse, and the flames were raging thick and high above us. Our orders were to carry the hose to the next gable, which was threatened by the fire. We had Just reached the point, our, elothes burnt off our backs, our faces singed, even our brass helmets crackling with the heat. Broadhurst was in front; we held the hose together and played it on the gable, which was beginning to smoke. "Will the whistle never blow ? ' he suddenly asked me. 'I can't hold out muclilonger.' "The boss won't forget us,' I answered. 'Hold tight, old chap.' "You see, we were '...standing on a ten-inch wall, and when Broadhurst fell I knew it was all up with him. "You'll see to Jack, mate,' he called out as he sank into the flames that raged below, "and before Heaven, Frank, I did my part by the lad. He was a kid of seven years when we took him in. Emily's mother was alive then, you know. The two children were brought up together, and a right merry little pair they were. Then at about his sixteenth year a change came over the boy. His father had always been a wild sort, and I suppose he got it from him; leastways, nothing would hold him in. He fair broke the poor wife's heart, for she had done a lot by him. I lectured him, I licked him, I did all that even a father could; but no, nothing would keep him back. He disappeared one Wednesday morning, and never a sight of him have I had since. Poor Emily was awfully down for a while; but she was young then, you know, and she got ,over it. When you began to come about the house, Frank, I think it did her a heap of good. But this man Abingdon has got a kind of way with him which would turn any girl's head. It has even bewitched myself,- and you know I'm not easily 'carried away." '"How do you rxplain it, Wales?" asked the younger of the two mt n. "ft s more than I can explain, Frank, except it be that there's something in Ralph Abingdon that kind of reminds me of Jack Broadhurst, who bolted from our house ten years ago. Heaven knows I did my part by the lad! We were always mates, his father and I, and if the boy was td, come back to-day I wouldn't turn him from my door. There is a warm corner in my heart for Broadhurst's son; aye, and there always will rte." • "Ys, yes," said Goodall; "that is all right, boss, but it don't alter the fact that Emily has married a man who comes from no one knows where, and of whom no man in London knows anything." s. "Tuts tuts, lad, you're still jealous! Take your defeat like a man. We can't win all the prizes in life. Make the beat of it." "Ah !" said Frank Goodell, with a sigh. "If only you knew how I felt ! lam alralotte in this world., never knew father nor mother, never knew anybody that I cared for much, until I came to your house and first sew Emily." The to men shook hands. Even the firm lips of the iron-hearted boss trembled.</p>
        <p>..... "Cheer up, Frank," he said, "the marriage has made a difference to me too, lad. She was all that I had, now that her mother is ,gone. I thought it was for the best. I would;not stand in the way of her happiness, and You must look at it that way too, Frank. She wished Abingdon, she sari she loves him, and we won't spoil two lives, will we, lad? There's duty to be done in the Whrld, and if fortune has been rough on you in this game, may be it will put metal into you. The man who has no one to care for and no one to care for him, is the chap I like for a mate. I feel easy about him in the time of danger. He will never abandon a mate, and, by Heaven! I will never abandon him." Once more the firm, strong hands were clasped together. At the 'same moment the fire alarm rang, and the next instant the whole station was in commotion. It was the beginning of Winter, and as the brigade dashed up the street the first snow-flakes were falling into the depths of the London fog. The alarm had come from a house near Long Acre, and when the engines arrived at the spofsthe fire had burst through the roof; thd building was doomed. A crowd had gathered in front, and as soon as the brigade drew up the people began to shout "Two men on the third flat ! " Immediately the fire-escape was brought along. It was set up to the window. Harry Wales and Frank Goodall made, the ascent. The flames were belching forth from the Sides of the building. The tenement was old, and burnt like matehwood. To reach the third flat, the boss and his. young follower hailto pass through living tongues ,of fire. Up and up they went, their hands blistered each time ihey grasped the rungs of the ladder. Up and up, while the people shouted below. Up and up, till their clothes were one mass of charcoal, their faces blackened with soot, till they were ahuost blinded -kith the smoke. At length they reached the window. One blow from Harry's axe shivered the pane, and the firemen leaped into the room. An awful sight presented itself. Two men were fighting like tigers. The Ceiling was crackling overhead, the flames were bursting through the floor ;but, stripped to the waist, with blood-shot eyes, a dagger in each right hand, the combatant, fought Harry Wales sprang between them. . . One of the combatants was Ralph Abingdon, his daughters huaband, the Other Was unknown to laiM. On the floor, Oiled by some villain's hand, lay Emilye</p>
        <p>what had brought his daughter there! That morning, as she and her husband sat at break, fast, Emily had observed that Ralph. Aibingdmi seemedlroubled by the receipt of a certain letter. "Anything wrong ? " she had asked. "No."' • • But half-an-hour later the letter was followed by its writer., a strange, uncouth indiviC7tal, who was shown into the rbom by Kitty; the maid. "Ah. Jack," exclaimed the stranger, "fc•rind you at last, old bk." • It.was evident to Mrs. Abingdon. that her husband Was by no means pleased with ihe greeting.. In fact, if these was any man in-the world whom ' Ralph Abingdon at that moment desired not to see it was his. old pal Tootal, who had thris intrnded hiesclf. Perhaps • Tootal felt -uopuori JRAO jpu (In no.i rfugunq uaaq I omit! eaaß nag jo OOU sytphUP,, ‘poinpitaxo Ott I • 'am.at oq. pusqsuti 15(0.13 mum u mud oil qiimo ouloulau 0(11</p>
      </div>
