        <p>In connection with the WHO SHOULD BE scandals of the South HANGED ? African war a a aying of</p>
        <p>Bing Solomon comes to mind: "01 making many books there is no end. Blue Books come out singly or in pairs, in a long succession, telling the long, saddening story of ineptitude, confused morals, and rank rascality. The accountants who have gone over the vast mass of disorderly papers left by the war continue the tale which was begun by Sir WILLIAM BUTLER'S Commission, and carried on by the Inquiry over which Mr. JUSTICE FARWELL pre- sided. Why have there been no prosecutions? Why is not somebody punished? Nay, why is not somebody banged? These are the impatient questions which rise to the lips as one peruses the reports ; and they aro followed by the reflection that the only punishment meted out was to a newspaper which spoke frankly about the scandals whilst they were still new, and when it was still possible by exposure to prevent their multiplication.</p>
        <p>There is more about the interesting firm of MEYER, Limited, in the latest Blue Books. We knew before how this company was biought into existence for the purpose of contracting for war stores, and how it made a very good thing of the business. We are now placed in possession of some, at least, of the details. There seems to have been hardly any capital at first, though nominally there was a capital of £'25,000. Really, however' little capital was needed to carry on business in the style that seems to have been common at military quarters in South Africa. The plan was to buy from the Army and sell back the same goods to the Army at an advanced rate. It was not necessary that the goods should be removed in all cases. They might be bought at a cheap rate and sold at a dear one—bought from tee Army and sold to the Army—without being so much as touched or inspected. And the persons responsible for the transactions on the military</p>
        <p>side were really so very accommodating. Where they sold goods they would spread the payments over convenient periods, and where they bought them back again they would pay money down. Capital was a superfluity, therefore. In three months Meyer, Limited, received nearly £62,000 more than they paid away. But even if they had I been required to pay down on the nail there were methods of getting money which involved no inconvenient calls on their shareholders. For instance, in April, 1904, they secured a contract which they immediately sold at a profit of £25,000 That account may be regarded as a present 'made to them, for no services rendered, by good- natured, simple-hearted JOHN BULL. The story unfolded in the latest Blue Books would be astonishing if it came upon us as a novelty. As matters stand, however, it inclines to become tedious, Goods belonging to the nation were sold cheap, as " surpluses," and the money received for them was put into private pockets. If there were no actual surpluses it was always , possible to make some by selling to the contractors what was needed by the Army. The corruption , was systematic. Officers took regular payments ' for betraying their trust and swindling their ' country. There is one who shines in comparison to some others because he took bribes without doing what was expected of him. An agent to one of the contractors writes about him : "A certain individual has been in the habit of receiving £lO a month from us. He is a useless old woman, and it is steer waste of money giving him anything." There is a pretty testi- monial of character ! The " certain individual," wearing the KING'S uniform, was above betraying his country by playing into the hands of rogues ; but he was not too proud to take the money which was intended to be the wages of roguery. It may be difficult to lay hands on the contractors whose mhthods of dealing are thus exposed. They do not find England the pleasantest of all possible places to live in, apparently,for they have gone to America, or elsewhere. But where hands can be laid upon them it should be done without delay. They were plainly guilty of conspiracy to defraud. As to the officers and others who were concerned in these shameful transactions, as representatives of the British Army, we shall trust MT. HALDANE to deal with them in a manner that will be remembered.</p>
        <p>Only a few days ago the SALVATION Salvationists of Warrington . .</p>
        <p>ARmy's Loss. were in high spirits over the . - • -</p>
        <p>visit to this 'town of their revered head and founder—General Booth. This week they have been plunged into mourning by the death of their esteemed and hard-working local leader—Adjutant Cooper—who passed away on Thursday after t very brief illness. The Adjutant was one of the large band of workers who joined so readily in extending a cordial welcome to General Booth and on the following morning, although he was really unfit to venture out of doors, he went as far as the Park Gates to shake hands with the General and wish him " God speed" on his tour. It is less than a year since Adjutant Cooper came to Warrington, but he had by his earnestness and devotion to duty won his way to the hearts of the poor amongst whom he laboured. He was a very diligent but unostentatious missioner and his place will be diffi- cult to fill.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the recent THE AMALGAMATION conference there is little OF LABOUR. ; sign at present of the amalgamation of the three or four organisations professing to represent Labour in Warrington. They are all anxious for consolidation in order to push forward Labour politics, but unfortunately each wants to draft the c(•distitution of the central authority in accordance with its own particular views. The Trades Coun- cil desires a trade union Labour Party, while the L.R.C.,with that precociousness which has always been its chief characteristic, demands that one authority shall take the place of those now exist- ing and adopt the L.R.C. constitution. Naturally there is a split, and a pretty severe one, on the constitutional rock. It is not clear by any means that the local trade unionists desire to bind themselves to one political party exclusive of the others, and this is the inherent difficulty in the way of the Trades Council proposal. Then there i‘s the L.R.C. con- stitution, which is sure to stick in the crop of the majority a long time before it is swallowed. What the ',L.P. thinks has not transpired, but presum- ably it will be on the spot when the final settle- ment is made. The question of funds, so far, has not been publicly mentioned, which appears to us rather significant. As a rule the trade unionists are jealous—and rightly so—of their funds, and whether they will ba prepared to contribute to a Central Labour Party composed of Trades Council delegates, L.R.C.'s and I.L.P.'s is a moot point. We should like to see Labour co-ordinated, and working with a single high purpose, but while so many factions claim to represent the workers the prospect of a satisfactory fusion of the units is not very bright.</p>
        <p>We are very pleased to THE BOROUGH see that the two present ACCOUNTS. elective auditors for the Borough of Warrington have a wider conception of their duties than their predecessors. For many years now the ratepayers of Warrington, and of most other towns, have had to be content with a certificate from the elective auditors guaranteeing merely the arithmetical accuracy of the Borough accounts and containing nothing in the way of criticism or comment. A few years ago there was a departure from prece- dent in Warrington when Mr. Alderman Bennett, ! then one of the Borough Auditors, ventured to ! say something unpalatable in his report, and it ! was held batk for some months by the Council. , The matter created quite a little scandal and was placed in the forefront of the non-trading agita- tion. Since then, elective auditors have done little but audit the accounts in formal fashion. Messrs. Bolton and England, the present auditors, have each taken the commendable step of making a critical report direct to the public in addition to giving the usual certificate of accuracy. Last week we published some straightforward criticisms by Mr. Bolton with regard to the spending of money without Local Government Board sanction, the administration of the Middleton Scholarship, and the breach of agreements by committees involving a loss to the ratepayers. Mr. England takes up the tale at greater length this week, and offers suggestions for administrative reforms which, we think, are worthy of consideration. For instance, as he points out, it w3uld be more businesslike and probably more economical if the accounts of the Gas Department were dealt with at Bank House, thus furthering the centralization idea which is prevalent in all up-to-date municipalities. Then, too, some. I thing might be saved in respect of horse-hire ! cartage,etc.,which total up to over £3OOO per year, ! if the Corporation undertook more of the work itself. These are matters which an intelligent auditor ought to bring before the public, as well as comparisons between 103. 6d. for three journeys to Manchester, and £1 us. 6d. for one journey to Bury, the sums charged respectively by two differ- ent members of the Corporation. Mr. England is very re-assuring as to the fivancial status of the Corporation, and as to the wisdom of the Cor- poration property-purchases during the past twenty years. There may be differences of opinion on these and other points, but at any rate the general body of ratepayers will feel grateful to these gentlemen for taking them into their con- fiderce on matters pertaining to the welfare of the municipality.</p>
        <p>A momentous matter for "CANTEEN." the seamen of the British Navy is about to be decided. An Admiralty Commission on Naval Canteens is shortly to make a tour of inspection, and upon its report will probably depend the continuance or otherwise of the present system. The canteen, whether naval or military, is always a fruitful source of discontent. Kipling somewhere or other has a reference to a military gentleman, "An' be kept canteen "—an expressive phrase the scorn- ful significance of which implies that little faith need be placed in that particular gentleman's honesty. The sailor-man, as a rule, has a suspicion that he la being " done " in the canteen. He regards a contractor with suspicion. He may be right or he may be wrong. Mr. Stewart Bowles gave the House of Commons some; specimens of shore and shipboard prices which argued that " Jack" is at least in some instances right in his attitude of hostility. At any rate, the Admiralty Commission will probably find a very firm conviction on the lower deck that the men should be allowed to keep the manage- ment of the canteens in their own hands. It is obvious that whatever the remedy be the present system does not give satisfaction, and that in some proportion " Jack "sutlers by it. Whether he could do the thing better himself is another question.</p>
