      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SOCIAL QUESTIONS AND</head>
        <p>SOCIAL QUESTIONS AND</p>
        <p>THEIR SOLUTION.</p>
        <p>[l3ll. W. R. LESTER, M.A.] Ex-President of the Scottish League for the Taxation of Land Values).</p>
        <p>IV.—THE TRUE REMEDY. We hope that no reformer will have come with us thus far without realising the first step which must be taken if anything permanent is to be accomplished in removing the hardships which beset a very large proportion of our industrial population. We may not all go so far as to say that drastic land reform would cure every social trouble, but previous chapters may at least have demonstrated that the first necessary . step is to secure to all equal right to use the earth, because not till we have got that have we a sound •' base on which to build. However it is not enough to demand land reform. That is much too vague. We .t want to make up our minds what kind of land reform, and then bow to get it. There are three possible courses. First, to declare that from a given date all land is to be taken over by the State (people) without payment, and to be let r and managed 'by the State. The objection to such procedure is the great suffer- jug which would be caused by the sudden and whole- sale transference, though, per contra, it might well be recalled that great suffering was, in fact, caused when ; the guilds, the Church, and the yeomen farmers were forcibly dispossessed of their lands without compensa- tion, and that they are therefore right in re-assuming possession here and now. But however that may be, it is quite certain that the people of this country show I not the slightest inclination to take such a drastic step. It would be waste of time to try to persuade ; them. 1" Second for the State to buy out present land-lords so that it might own and manage the land itself. The /- objection to this is that the public would be buying what, in reality, is already its own. There is in fact A but one landowner—the Crown or people. Masses of men who are already suffering from the injustice of having to pay private rent for use of the earth would have a second wrong inflicted on them if forced to buy out existing holders. Our postulate is that every human being has an equal right to benefit by the free ' bounties of Nature, all laws and customs to the contrary notwithstanding. This cannot be secured without equal right of access to land. The great mass is now wronged by our denial of this fundamental natural right : they would be doubly wronged were they forced to .purchase it from those who have deprived them of it. Another , objection to any scheme of land nationalisation is that it would involve State management. An enormous State Land Office would have to be established vastly greater than any existing State department, and we - would find ourselves face to face with all the abases, expenses and red tape inherent in bureaucracy. Third to tax land -values with the view to ultimately establishing the single tax. This, in our opinion, is undoubtedly the method at once most effective, mos° sure, and most easily secured, because already well within the region of practical legislation, and causing a minimum of loss to those who profit by existing institutions. It is a proposal backed by some COO of our leading municipalities and rating authorities. The moral basis underlying the taxation of land values is this. All men are born with equal right to land. If then, we grant to some exclusive right to use portions ef the earth they ought to compensate their dispossessed fellows. The former having secured the privilege of peculiarly profiting from Nature's bounty should have to pay for their privilege in proportion to its value, i.e., in proportion to the advantage they have gained at the expense of others. The expropriated . (non-landlords) thus receiving full compensation for the right they had surrendered, would be placed on a . footing of equality with the landowners. In a word those whom we call landowners should pay rent to the State, that rent being used in support of public services. Equal rights to use of land would be thus secured , and all would have a fair start in the race for life because all would have equal opportunity of satisfying their desires. That constitutes the single tax. But however desirable it might be to promptly establish the single tax it is morally certain that it will not come in one step. We shall not be able to get full cora- pensation from landowners all at once. The practical measure resolves itself into what is • popularly known as the taxation of land values' —that is to say, the compensation demanded , from landowners will as a beginning be only a percentage of the full values of their land •, this percentage to be increased as occasion offers till ulti- mately the single tax will be secured, and in the same act all labour products will be untaxed, land values , being more than sufficient to meet all public wants. It will thus he seen that taxing land values is a means only to an end, the end being the untaxing of industry (now so heavily taxed), the freeing of labour, and the securing of equal opportunity to all. The objections to the other two schemes are also removed, for by gradually increasing the tax on land values no ques- tion either of confiscation or compensation to present holders can arise. But how, it will be asked, by taxing land values are we going to touch pressing social problems like unemployment and housing ? The answer is that in! this way we shall set the machine going which will ultimately overthrow those great twin obstacles to the production and proper distribution of wealth—land monopoly and a vicious system of taxation. You say it is a small instrument to attain such great result. Yes, but it goes to the root. It vas Newton who said, '- " Give me a fulcrum and I will move the world." This is the fulcrum by use of which the whole structure of society may be changed for the better. It is clear that were every owner of land compelled , to develop his property to the utmost, or else allow someone else to do so,production would increase. And if at the same time the two hundred odd millions of taxes with which we now burden trade and wages were removed the stimulus to trade and relief of the labourer would be greater still. Well, that is pre-. ' cisely what would happen. Every landowner being under compulsion to pay a tax proportional to the unimproved value of the laud he held (that is, its value apart from any structure or work of man on it) would be actively spurred on to make - the very most of his land so as to get from it the greatest possible revenue out of which to pay the tax. Much valuable land would thus be brought into use or driven to better use than at present, and land cannot be developed without mea*. finding employment, for there is no form of industry i -- which s not based on use otland. At the same time, , neither land-owners nor others would be called on to pay a single penny on any building or other improve- ments they might make. In effect, the community would say to landlords "We cede to you exclusive use, of these natural opportunities for employment, but, at the same time, we are going to so arrange that it will be to your interest to make the very best use of them. Go ahead and we will altogether abandon the present stupid system of taxing you and other people on what you produce,"</p>
        <p>(To be concluded). A public meeting will be held in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Thursday evening next to discuss "Social Questions and their Solution by means of the Taxation of Land Values." The principal speakers will be the writer of the above series of articles, Mr. W. R. Lester, M.A., Mr. Spirrow (Bradford), and Mr. McHugh (Birkenhead.)</p>
      </div>
