      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>DANCING MADNESS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, whilst Mr. Finlow, green- grocer, of Manchester and Widnes, was unloading some bunches of bananas that had come from Jamaica, at Widnes siding, he found in one of the bunches an insect which is known as the tarantula spider, which was secured and placed in a tin for safety, to prevent its getting away. The spider is enormous, and its sting is stated to produce acute dancing Itnadness. The insect found at Widnes is a fine specimen of the spider, measuring three and a half inches in jength,and possess- ing ten legs. In consequence of the danger attached to the possibility of these venemous insects getting among the fruit, dealers and those who handle imported bananas exercise great care when any discovery of this character is made. It was afterwards chloroformed and placed in a bottle of preservative. WIDNES CORPORATION INSPECTION.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the members of the Widnes Corporation made their annual inspection of the Corporation properties. Leaving the Town Hall in motor-cars, landaus, and other vehicles, the party visited the Accident Hospital, the Victoria Promenade, the Gas- works, the Infectious Diseases Hospital, the Victoria Park, the Cemetery, and the waterworks at Stock's Well and Netherley. The Accident Hospital is the only one in England supported out of the borough rates. Sugges- tions for enlarging the institution were considered and favourably commented upon. At the gasworks the process of making "the cheapest gas in the world "was explained by Mr. Isaac Carr, the engineer. The party examined the excavations for the proposed huge gasometer and the rest of the up-to-date plant which is being erected at a cost of £12,000. At the Stock's Well Waterworks the new boreholes were found to be com- pleted, and the party inspected the concrete beds which are being made for the modern engines to be erected. The additional plant here is to cost £14,000. During the day a minor accident occurred, one of the coun- cillors getting his fingers badly trapped in a carriage door.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Messrs. T. Baxter (presiding). T. Wright, S. Woodward, C. E. Linaker, T. Wood, C. Roylance, J. W. Temperley, J. Hallows, H. Eyes J. Spruce, J. Whitley, J. Bolshaw, J. E. Harrison, J. H. Davies, J. Douglas, T. Antwis, G. Pilling, G. Egerton Warburton, A. Broome, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk). J. Cunnah (workhouse master), T. Cook, W. R. de Prez and J. Pearson (relieving officers). A QUESTION OP SUPERANNUATION. Mr. Baxter moved "That Mrs. Eliza Hayes be repaid the sum of £ll 6s. 6d. the amount paid by her as a percentage of her salary towards superannuation and that she also be paid £l5 15s. as a recompense for the loss of her situation, and that the sanction of the Local Government Board be requested thereto." Mr. Roylance thought Mrs. Hayes should be presented' with at least £25. _ _</p>
        <p>All is not well in the Southport Conservative camp. Mr. Samuel Brighouse, the county coroner, has just resigned the chairmanship of the Conservative Associa- tion, owing it is said, to criticisms which have appeared in the local Conservative press. The incident has caused quite a sensation in the town Where Mr. Brighouse is so well-known as a political tghter. * * * There are two very good reasons for satisfaction • that the Police Sports are over. One is that the arts were a success, and should benefit the charities iderablv and the other is that the man in the street .1 be free for another twelve-month from the enthu- 4astic " P.C." who insists on either selling him a ticket or locking him up. The local Education accounts, which are issued RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. Nvith the Borough Treasurer's abstract of the Corpora- I The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- lieu accounts, make interesting reading. On ele— trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when , laentary education the expenditure during the year there were present—Messrs. G. Pilling (presiding), T. £34,573 us, 4d. the largest item being £20,562 Baxter, S. Wright, S. Woodward, C. E. Lina.ker, C. ,r,a3 Roylance, J. Hallows, H. Eyes, J. Spruce, J. Whitley, J. 6d. for teachers' salaries. Higher education cost D olshaw, J. E. Harrison, J. H. Davies, T. Antwis, G. ,'41790 Is. ld., secondary school teachers' salaries vtgerton Warburton, A. Broome, G.F. Ashton (clerk), talling ;£1428 15s. 2d. while the amount paid to .E. Hough (assistant clerk),J.Farrington (inspector), ,levening school teachers was 5,1957 13s. Sd. The J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), and Dr. Adams 'aMount raised from the Borough rate for education (medical officer). Was £11,843 17s. 4d. MOTOR CAR TRAFFIC. * * * 1 The Clerk to the Cheshire County Council forwarded for approval the following resolution : decided The fact that the Manchester City Council has "That this report til of the to record its disappciatme