        <p>The question was adjourned to allow for the altera- tion of the amount to the sum suggested by Mr. Roylance.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF DEPUT,)TIONS. Messrs. J. Whitley and J. H. Davies were appointed to visit the Pantasaph Orphanage, Messrs. Woodward and Pilling, to the Liverpool School for the Blind, and Messrs. Wright, Eyes and Harrison to the County Asylum.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF OUT RELIEF. The Finance Committee reported payment for out- relief to Mr. Cook of £7O, to Mr. de Prez £2B and to Mr. Pearson £3O.</p>
        <p>g was wrong with the mete-r an extensive- search is made for it at first,without success. The Rev. J. ' inning was called upon to assist and after above half hour had been wasted it was found carefully stowed iy in the cellar. Still there was no light and after experiments to induce the meter to perform its function an adjournment was made to an le-room at the Chapel where the meeting suffered 'further interruption. * 5 * Blackflies and midges have been more than a little iblesome during the hot spells of this summer, but pared with the visitation which has fallen upon I'rich-by-the-sea their advent has been a pleftsant ity in the monotonous round of every-day tee. On Tuesday the small black winged ants 4 Sandwich not in single file but in big battalions. spread like a cloud over the town and made 7 place look black where they settled. People in streets had an exceedingly uncomfortable time, as - insects stung badly. The quarter worst affected 4;44 Sandwich-street, Strand-street, and the district I,,(nincl the river Stour. Every inch of the pavements 'Las thickly covered with the insects like a carpet, and '44 broad river itself was black with them right to the aary. we may be thankful that in this district, NO three in one eye at one: is about the maximum ! * * * zefka correspondent of the " Manchester Guardian " to tttes a funny incident which will appeal strongly to 4,4e:14 anti-ritualist. He was in conversation with an .I,zent villager who delivered himself as follows : our fresh parson, now," he went on, growing l'tle fiercer. " Put me' ead in t' church an Sunday in', and wot do I see? Cangles (candles), young .cangles ! " " A bit ritualistic," I suggested. svil-ke bit ! Now, I'll tell you the story I told t' parson 'n come to see me. A boy went to t' grocer's in I _4Y-time, I says, to fetch some things for 'is mother. 'rant cangles,' says 'e. °angles ! ' says the shop- , :1:Vot do yer want wiv cangles in the day- What did the parson say to that ? ' I " Say ? " returned my friend, with the radiant 'I a victor. " • It's a fine afternoon,' he says."</p>
        <p>" from reveilld to last post." DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION AT HAYDOCK. A contest to fill the vacancy in West Ward, caused by the acceptance of the medical officership of the town- ship by Dr. Dowling, one of the members of the Haydock Council, took place on Monday. The candi- dates were Messrs. H. Griffin and Samuel Platt. Mr. Griffin had been a member of the council six years previous to retiring four years ago, but Mr. Platt won be 15 votes. The result was declared by Mr. Joseph Dickinson, clerk to the District Council, as follows : Platt, 242 ; Griffin, 227 ; majority for Mr. Platt, 15. THIRTY-FIVE YEARS A HEADMASTER. ( Mr. Alfred Smith, headmaster, and Mrs. Smith, head mistress at the Parkside Mills Schools, Golborne, were on Monday the recipients of an illuminated address and purse of gold, subscribed by the residents as an acknow- ledgement of their services on retirement. Councillor Abbott presided. Mr. and Mrs. Smith had been associ- ated with the school for thirty-five years. Mr. Smith, in acknowledgment, remarked that few teachers lived long enough to retire on superannuation. Many were the speeches appreciative of Mr. and Mrs. Smith's work, educationally and socially, in the neighbourhood. EARLESTOWN GROCER'S ASSOCIATION. A large company of grocers and tradespeople left Earlestown last Thursday for Southport, it being the general holiday for the tradespeople. The party travelled by saloon, which was provided for out of the funds of the Association and was much appreciated by those taking advantage of it. The ladies and non- smokers were provided with a.good assortment of choice fruit at the expense of the president, Mr. R. Molyneux. The party dispersed at Southport into different com- panies to enjoy themselves in their own way, there being no fixed rule to spend the day. The return Journey was started at 7-3, arriving home about 9.30. It was said that the outing had been" full of harmony" land that much credit was due to Messrs. 11. Molyneux and E. A. Ince for their efficient arrangements. LODGER'S THEFT. At the Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday, a youth named George Frederick Hanks was charged with stealing a silver lever watch, watch chain, medal, coat and vest, silk muffler and cap, valued at £2 10s., the proper of Aaron Stock, Broad-lane, Buttonwood. Superin- tendent Strickland stated that the prisoner formerly lodged with Stocks. At 9.30 on the night of December 27th, Mrs. Stocks saw the articles safe in the house. At six o'clock the following morning she missed them and the prisoner had decamped. Information was given to the police, and on Saturday night, prisoner gave himself up to the St. Helens police.—Evidence was given by Mrs. Stocks and P.C. Yates, the latter stating that prisoner, in reply to the charge, said " That is right. '—Prisoner was remanded to Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>!DRILLERS' STRIKE AT EARLESTOWN. The drillers, numbering over a hundred, employed at , the Vulcan Foundry, Earlestown, on Tuesday handed in i their notices to cease work at the end of fourteen days. The Vulcan Foundry is one of the greatest locomotive- building works in the kingdom, at which more than a thousand men are employed. The drillers are paid at the rate of one guinea a week, and the men's applica- tion was for an advance of 3s. a week. It was claimed that at another works in the district 28s. a week is paid for similar work. On Friday the men asked for the advance to be given, but this was declined, and on Saturday a meeting was held, at which a deputation of , two was appointed. These men met the management ,on Monday. That night a meeting was celled, and the deputation reported the result of the interview, end the outcome, is that the men have handed in their notices. Some of the men are in a trade union, but 1 whether the strike will affect the whole shop was a i moot point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>REGISTRATION WORK AT ST. HELENS. Mr. D. Wilson, the agent jar the Labour party in St. Helens, has made 420 claims for new votes and in respect of voters omitted from the lists. The Labour party have made no objections whatever, and the Conserva- tives are in this respect following the example set last year. The revision court will be.held at the Town Hall during the second week in September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LAUDANUAI POISONING.</head>
        <p>LAUDANUAI POISONING.</p>
        <p>Matthew Blackmore, a collier, of Tickle-street, was found in Liverpool-road on Friday night at eleven o'clock, by Sergeant Ackers. He told the officer that be had been taking laudanum,and that it was all through his mother. He wanted to cut the thread of life because he was tired of living. His mother had pawned his clothing and boots, and he had bought three-penny- worth of laudanum. An emetic was administered, and he recovered. The Chief Constable said that the man had been drinking, but what he stated about his mother seemed to be true. He was a widower. The magis- trates said that Blackmore did not seem to have the courage to put his affairs into a proper state, and he would be remanded for a week, to give him a chance of getting fairly sober.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDES AT ST. HELENS. At St. Helens on Saturday, a couple of charges of attempted suicide were heard. In the first case Bridget Mannion, a married woman of Mount-street, was charged with trying to hang herself. It appears that on Friday night, about seven o'clock, when she was in the house with one of her young daughters, she got up from the sofa, where she had been resting, and sat up, tied a clothes line three times round her neck, and then swung herself oil. The child tied to prevent her doing this, but when she saw her mother was hanging she ran and fetched Robert Bowes, who lived in the back part of , the house, and he cut her down, being just in time to prevent her death. The woman said, when charged, that , her husband was to blame, but she herself had been having some drink. She was remanded to Walton for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WORKS MANAGER ASSAULTED.</head>
        <p>WORKS MANAGER ASSAULTED.</p>
        <p>• Peter Ross, a young lead worker, of 26, Elliott-street, who had just returned from his Militia training, was convicted at St. Helens, on Monday, of assaulting James D. Wanless, works manager at Messrs. Quirk, Barton, and Co.'s lead works.—Mr. E. W. Swift pro- secuted, and said the assault was of a serious nature. During July a series of lead and copper thefts were investigated by the police, and Ross and a man named Alcock were arrested. Ross turned King's evidence, and Alcock went to the assizes for trial. In consequence of statements by the police, a man named Martin Salmon, uncle of Ross was discharged from the works by Mr. Wanless. On Monday, July 30, Ross was in St. Helens in his Militia uniform, when he met Mr. Wanless and struck him a couple of violent blows in the throat and butted him in the face.—Ross was sent to gaol for a month's hard labour, this term to follow a previous sentence of two months' for theft.—Ross was also charged with assaulting Mrs. Catherine Clarke' a shopkeeper, on Saturday last.—He was bound oyer for twelve months, with the alternative of seven day-s' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>COLONEL W. L. PILKINGTON FACETIOUS.</head>
        <p>COLONEL W. L. PILKINGTON FACETIOUS.</p>
        <p>, NEW CONSERVATIVE CLUB AT ST. HELENS. I A new Workingmen's Conservative Club was opened on Tuesday, at Denton's-green, St. Helens, by Colonel W. Lee Pilkington. The club is part of the scheme of the association to provide centres of political activity in all parts of the town. It is situated at the junction of Windleshaw-road and Greenfield-road, and has been equipped and presented to the association by Colonel Richard Pilkington, C. 8., ex-member for the Newton Division. Mr. J. Grace presided at the opening, when there was a crowded attendance—Colonel Pilkington , spoke at some length on local politics. The number of promises that were made in St. Helens at the last election were something astounding. If any of the electors had asked for a railway to the moon it would have been freely promised. (Laughter.) That club and the other organizations of the association were intended to keep the workingmen informed as to what was going on in the political world, and to enable them to distinguish between the realities and the shams, be- tween specious promises and reliable perforraances,and he trusted it would have a long and useful career.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ST. HELENS STREET OUTRAGE.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS STREET OUTRAGE.</p>
        <p>The perpetrator of the outrage on a young lady at St. Helens, has been discovered by the police, and John Reece, a labourer, who lives in the model lodging- house of the town, was charged on Saturday with frequenting Denton's Green-lane, and also With an aggravated assault on Miss Fanny Duckworth.—The evidence was that ou Saturday night, the 11th inst., when Miss Duckworth was going home to Kiln-lane, a man rushed out of Chadwick's-entry, loading from Keswick-road, and put his arms round her neck and almost strangled her. He dragged her some twelve yards into the entry, and when he re- leased her throat somewhat she was able to scream,, and a little girl came on the scene. She was followed by a man and a policeman. Detective Curran made inquiries, and on Friday night arrested the prisoner at the rear of the lodging-houses in Sandfield-crescent. When he was charged with the two offences, Reece said :—"I took hold of her, intend- ing to have a kiss, and, having taken hold of her, I tried to pull her into the entry. She screamed, and I ran away."—The magistrates said they would give the prisoner what he deserved. They could not have a man like him hanging about the town doing such terrible things, and for the assault he would have to go to prison for six months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</head>
        <p>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</p>
        <p>The District Officers paid official visits to Court " Jaaziel 138 "at the White Bull, also " Warrington 430 "at the Blue Lion, on Saturday August 18th. District Chief Ranger Bro. Peter Peacock was invited to take the chair, and D.S.C.R. Bro. Joseph Owen occupied the vice-chair, and. District Secretary Bro. Davies was present at both meetings. The chairman, Bro. Peacock, gave a very interesting and instructive report of the proceedings at the High Court, Rings Lynn. daring August Bank Holiday week, pointing out the various debates he found it necessary to take part in, on behalf of the Warrington District particularly with respect to the drastic changes which, were proposed in the constitutional and democratic management of the Order. Bro. Peacock knowing this would debar the working men from continuing in the management of the affairs of the Order at headquarters opposed the alteration, as far as it ley in his power, and as the amendments were defeated by an' over- whelming majority it was very satisfactory to the Warrington District. The amendment proposing the appointment of public Auditor § for the accounts of the Order was also strenu- ously opposed by Bro. Peacock and after thoroughly discussing the matter, the amendment war defeated.</p>
        <p>Brothel' PEICOCK, tv' hen the accounts wernVaiii'dis- cussed, found it necessary to protest against the unbusinesslike methods in which some of the obstruc- tionist members desired to question and discuss item per item of the 01 pages of matter, and after two hours had been vvasted upon 4 pages, he proposed, and it was carried that the whole of the remaining 57 pages of accounts be confirmed, as certified by the auditors. This caused a great commotion, whichhas been reported in the papers, but it is hoped it will have the effect of reducing discussion on finance to a more sensible manner at future High Court meetings. Several other important questions were debated during the week, amongst which was a proposal from other Friendly Societies that they should decline to pay sick allowances to members in receipt of compensation under the Workmen's Liability Act,bnt as the Order con- tracted to pay allowances to members,they resolved it was undesirable to recognise the request, and in any case they should pay sick allowances to our members, whether obtaining compensation or otherwise, in accordance with their obligations to the members. Altogether the proceedings were very instructive, and made enjoyable by the hospitality of the leading gentry of King's Lynn. At the conclusion of the address, Bro. Peacock was heartily thanked by the members assembled at the two Courts, and haying suitably replied, the meeting terminated.</p>
        <p>-- At a representative meeting of the various officers held on Tuesday evening, under the presidency of Bro. Joseph Owen, D.S.C.R. it was arranged that the annual picnic should take place on Saturday, September Bth, to Belle Vue. A strong committee was formed, District Chief Ranger, Bro. Peacock being appointed secretary, and P.D.C.R. Bro. J. Chrimes treasurer. Particulars of trains, etc., will be advertised later.</p>
        <p>It was reported on Thursday to the Prestwich *Union Board of Guardians that no fresh facts had been gleaned as to the circumstances under which a pauper lunatic, named Cohen, bad sustained ten broken ribs prior to his death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
