      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MARCONI S NEW ROLE.</head>
        <p>MARCONI S NEW ROLE.</p>
        <p>INVENTOR OF WIRELESS TELEGRAPHY AS GRAPHOPHONE EXPERT.</p>
        <p>The announcement is made that Mr. Marconi has become interested in the talking machine and intends to devote his ingenuity and inventive genius towards its development. It is predicted that the greatest inventor of the pre- sent generation, whose great achievements have been in the realm •of the transmission of sound, will have many brilliant ideas on the subject of the recording and reprOducing of sound which will result in inventions of great practical utility. In this connection it is interesting to recall that the talking machine of to-day owes its very life to the inventions of Alexander Graham hell, the inventor of the telephone. The greatest minds in this sphere of science have been deeply interested in its principles, so that it is not strange that Marconi should associate himself as chief consulting physicist of the Columbia Phonograph Company, the owners of Professor Bell's fundamental patents in the art of recording and repro- ducing sound waves. A recent invention in sound production covers the discovery of a new principle of reinforcing or magnify- ing the sound vibrations so that the tone is augmented to an astonishing degree. This principle has found i practical application n the Columbia sound magnify- ing graphophone which reproduces an ordinary recor d , with sixteen times the volume that would be given by the same record on a machine not fitted with the new device. There are great possibilities in it, and while , Mr. Marconi has not, of course, disclosed what his views are on this and other talking machine ideas, experi- ments are to he conducted in the Columbia Cognpany's laboratories in England and America, and interesting developments in machines and records may be biked , for.</p>
        <p>A great crowd is expected at the Women's Suffrage demonstration in Stevenson-square, Manchester, on Sunday afternoon to welcome Miss Annie Kenney on her release from Holloway Gaol. There will be three platforms, and all the speakers will be women.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FUTURE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE FUTURE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED AMALGAMATION UNDER CONSIDERATION. --- Labour Representation Committee's Demands.</p>
        <p>The La 42 our forces of Warrington are at present seriously considering the advisability of forming one central body to discharge the functions of the three or , four authorities now in existence. Unless, however, the local Labour Representation Committee moderate ' their demands in regard to the constitution of the pro- posed new organization there is little probability of anything in the way of centralization being effected. The Committee demand, in effect, that all labour bodies shall cease to exist and their places shall be taken by one authority which shall adopt the constitution of the Labour Representation Committee. This, to some, and particularly to the Trades Council was not acceptable andthough nothing definite has been decided there is he probability that the Trades Council will reject the proposal and consider the advisability of altering their rules so as to permit of political work being undertaken.] Reports on the conference which was recently held were presented at the meeting of the Trades Council on Tuesday evening. Councillor STOTT explained that the conference was well attended, thirteen out of fifteen delegates appointed being present. The meeting was unanimously of opinion that there should be one central 1 labour organization in Warrington instead of three or four, as tending to prevent the wastage of money in administration expenses while practically little work was being done for the labour movement. The question then arose as to whether the new organiza- tion should be purely trade union. The delegates from the Trades Council were under the impression that the Council desired that this should be the case whilst the other delegates present dessented from that view. On a vote being taken it was decided that the central authority should be open to trade unions and reform I societies which agreed with the objects of the labour movement and which were connected with no political party other than the labour party. The discussion took place on the question as to whether the new authority should be on a broad basis—to go outside trade union I branches and to admit organizations such as the Co-operative Society and the Independent Labour Party. Some delegates were of opinion that it would be much better and would be accepted with much better grace by the rank and file of the trade union movement if the organization was kept purely trade union. The repre- sentatives of the Trades Council held that view but were defeated by eight votes to four. Mr. PLIN`sTON said they had a clear issue before them. Personally he was not prepared to do anything which might damage the existing strength of the Trades Council. They were prepared to go on with the trade union work of the Council and to go in for a forward movement in regard to municipal matters and municipal representation, but they found that another section represented at the conference were determined to have the conditions under which their organization worked adopted as a definite principle in any organization which might be formed, and the Trades Council delegates were defeated. They were all agreed that something required to be done to brighten the future of labour in Warrington. Then they came to the narrow dividing line. The Trades Council delegates were not prepared to bind themselves to something which would perhaps result in their doing even less work than was being done at the present time. He said, with all due deference to the Labour Repre- sentation Committee that up to the present the Labour Representation Committee had not done that work for which it was formed. The Ttades Council delegates felt that rather than do something which might damage the prospects of the Trades Council it would be better for them to remain as they were with a little more for- ward movement.</p>
        <p>Mr. PODMORE said that whilst he was in favour of a central body, and whilst personally he was in favour of a forward movement by all labour forces which pledged themselves for labour pure and simple, he felt that he would not be doing his duty if he voted against the wishes of that Council which were for a trade union organization which would unify the political and trade union forces of the town. It seemed to him that if any action was proposed which might damage the Trades Council they had better not take it. Of course the ultimate and final decision rested with the branches and for them the issue was clear. The resolution which was carried, was that they should form a central body exactly on the same lines as the National Labour Party—to admit all bodies pledged to independent labour represeetation—and the question was : Were the trade unionists of Warrington prepared to take such a step ? (" No.") If they were prepared to take that step all well and good, but if they were not and withheld in large numbers from an organization of that description the position of labour would be worse than it was at that time. He was prepared to place the matter before the rank and file of trade unionists to decide whether they were in favour of a trade union political organization. This would have to be on independent lines. Their unions in the past had been so divided between Liberal and Tory that the only 1 straight course was to be independent. If they were not prepared it was better that they should stop as they were.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLumn said he thought some of the delegates who attended the Conference had biassed minds and had previously decided upon a certain course of action. , They were told that not one iota of the Labour Repre- sentation Committee's programme Would be relinquished and, practically, that unless they were prepared to drop the Trades Council altogother and to affiliate with the Labour Representation Committee nothing could come of the conference. The constitution of the Labour Representation Committee it was pointed out had been a failure—and he contended that it had been a failure— but notwithstanding this the delegates from that body persistently urged that their constitution must be observed or otherwise they would not be a party to the formation of a centralised body. The reports from the delegates were approved. Mr. SMITH :It appears to me that the independent Labour Party want to take all the honours—that is if there are any. Mr. PLUMB: The Independent Labour Party had nothing to do with the conference. Several other members also protested against the independent Labour Party being mentioned and for a time Mr. Smith could not proceed. At last he managed to interject" Go on. Show your ignorance by inter- rupting., Mr. Smith was proceeding with his speech when Mr. Grealey jumped up and moved the deferring of the discussion until the next meeting of the Council. For a time the meeting was in a state of disorder but eventually Mr. SMITH was allowed to proceed. He asked if they weregoing to their branches to tell the members that they must not belong to any political party? Two political parties had been mentioned but he maintained that there were more and he protested against so much political work going on in the Trades Council. When he first joined the Ootincil anything of the kind was hushed in an instant but they were getting worse and %Tine.</p>
        <p>-PODMORE : As the delegates were sent by this Council to take up political work Mr. Smith's protest comes too late.</p>
        <p>Mr. GREALEY moved and Mr. HEALEY seconded the adjournment of the discussion. Mr. Comm); urged that the matter was much too important to be adjourned. He would like to know whether the Trades Council representatives at the conference were not as biassed as any other delegates ? Mr. GREALEY'S motion was then put to the meeting' and adjourned. The question of fighting wards at the coming Novem- ber election was also postponed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ORDINARY BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>ORDINARY BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'MASSEY was elected to represent the Council at a conference to be held in Liverpool on September Ist under the auspices of the Workmens National Housing Reform Council :—ln answer to Mr. Horrocks, Mr. Corrin said it was not for the Labour Representative Committee but for the affiliated societies to decide what wards, if any, should be contested at the coming November elections.—Mr. Moordeld, assurance agents delegate, gave notice that at the next meeting of the Council he would move "calling attention to the shame- ful conditions under which the assurance agents of Warrington are working at the present time Rnd asking for the sympathy and co-operation of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council to assist the members of the Nati ono' Life Assurance Agents in their efforts to secure the standard rate of commission adopted by the National Union."—The representative of the Shop Assistants Union gave notice that he would move that Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P., be asked to vote for the inclusion in the Bill before the House of Commons of compensation for all shop •.ssistants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
      </div>
