      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BOROUGH ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE BOROUGH ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER ELECTIVE AUDIfOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>CRITICISMS AND SUGGESTIONS FROM MR. E. ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>j Mr. E. England. one of the borough elective auditors has submitted the following report to the Mayor, ! Aldermen, Councillors and Burgesses of the County Borough of Warrington. i Gentlemen,—Along with'my colleagas Mr. Councillor Wilson and Mr. Laurence Bolton I have audited and examined the accounts, books, invoices, vouchers and documents of the various departments of the Corpora- tion (Education Department excepted) from Ist April 1905 to the 31st March, 1901 lam pleased to report that I find same, not only in order, but that they are . kept in a manner which is highly creditable to the staff I engaged. The various transactions are easily to be traced. I feel it, however, my duty to you and the ratepayers to further report and submit to you the following : THE GAS DEPARTMENT. I am of opinion, an opinion I know shared by others, , who are conversant with the working of this depart- ment, that part of the book-keeping is superfluous. ! The sending out of the Bills and the collection and " receiving of the accounts should be merged into and I managed at the Corporation Offices is Sankey-street. I must point out to you that not only would a large economy be effected by this arrangement, but that it would also be more convenient to the general body of I ratepayers. I may further point out to you that the merging and concentration of all departments is now being adopted by other Corporations with considerable advantages and I trust that Xarrington will not be any longer behind- hand in this respect. You have already adopted the system so far as the Poor Rate, Improvement Rate, Water Rate and electricity accounts are cos- cerned, therefore why not the gas accounts as well ? It may be news to some also, when I state that the slot meter consumers pay at the rate of 2s. 10d. per 1,000 cubic feet as against 2s. gd.for ordinary consumers. It is true that there is more expense in connection with the slot meters, yet seeing that the slot meter consumer " prepays " I think it may fairly be claimed he is entitled to further consideration. If my memory serves me correctly, the number of the slot meters in use is between 10,000 and 11,000 ! I think it only fair to add that in many respects the gas department is kept in a highly efficient manner and that those in charge of it are to be congratulated on its prosperous condition. WATER DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>Owing to the present incomplete position of matters Ido not in this report propose to offer any cliticism on the department, except to point out that items Investigation Committee expenses (£lO3 ss. 31) and report c., re Lawson's Contract (£274 3s. Sd.) charged against Borough Rate Revenue account should for correct account keeping and other purposes have been charged against this department.</p>
        <p>PROPERTY PURCHASING. I have examined the deeds and ascertained the sums paid for the various properties bought by the Corpora- tion during the past 20 years. I era of opinion that on the whole, the purchases are judicious ones, and in the best interests of the town. These properties have not only included those required for street widening purposes, but also many "slum" properties, which very properly have been and are being demolished.</p>
        <p>DEPUTATIONS. - Although I am bound to acknowledge that good results are•obtained by visiting other towns for the purposes of acquiring information, yet, I am of opinion these visits are apt to be overdone and that the expenses charged are in some cases excessive. I think it is only right to say that there are one or two bright exceptions for I find that Mr. Councillor James Evans only charged 10s. Gd. for 3 journeys to and from Manchester. This compares very favourably with one gentleman who received 11s. Gd. for one journey to and from Bury. London is of course the chief place of visit, but I am under the impression that a few of these journeys could be dispensed with. Items of over £7 and £3 for three days are apt to mount up. There appears to be a lack of system too, so far as the members of the Council are concerned, but there is a uniform one so far as the officials are concerned.</p>
        <p>PAVING OF PRIVATE STREETS AND PASSAGES, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The sum of £5,1.15 2s. 61. has been paid' during the year in respect of these. The criticisms of the Medical Officer of Health and of the Press on the condition of the streets and passages so far as it affected the health of the public, have evidently borne fruit.</p>
        <p>HORSE HIRE, CARTAGE, CONVEYANCES, &amp;c. In going through the various accounts, one cannot help being struck with the largo amount paid for the hire of horses, carts and cabs. Two departments alone have snant £1,199 12s. 103. and £l,OOO 13s. 3L respec- tively for these items and altogether over £3,000 is, spent year after year in this respect. To those who understand finance they will easily see that £3,000, capitalised to a Corporation, means a very large amount. I have therefore come to the conclusion that not only coald a considerable sum be saved to the town but employment found under superior conditions than at present exists for men who would become Corporation employs. In addition considerable time would be I saved as well to various departments in preparing I specifications &amp;c., for work required to be done, and, I also a good deal of labour saved to the Council in con- sidering tenders and accepting contracts. The work Iwould also be better and moreefficiently done. In referring to this may I say that in my opinion the. Council are to be commanded for having adopted the principle of a minimum wage for their employs. ICouncillor Stott is to be congratulated on having had I the moral courage to introduce the matter and Mr. I Councillor 13arton for subsequently supporting it. It is in my opinion one of the brightest episodes of the year.</p>
        <p>THE FINANCIAL S FAT US OF THE CO it PORA.TION The financial position of the Corporation is.. , undoubtedly sound. It has been my lot and privilege for many years to have been in close touch with public men and public offices of other towns, and I have no hesitation in saying that the Warrington Corporation financially compares very favourably with others. Most of the undertakings are in a remunorative con- dition and various economies have been effected. The policy of your predecessors, and of some of you therefore, in purchasing these and also in introducing • new undertakings and thus bringing them under public control and management instead of being in private hands, has this proved to have been a wise and sound one and has contributed greatly in many ways, to the prosperity of the town. In conclusion may I add that as I consider myself to I be like yourselves a servant of the ratepayers, I am, in- addition to sending this report to you, furnishing a copy to the local press,—l remain yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>E. ENGLAND; Elective Auditor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church, Preachers 10.30,Rev. S. Pitt, 6.30 Mr. J. Upson. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, preachers 10.30 Rev, W. %tenor, 6.30 Rev. J. Manning. Wycliffe Congregational Church. During renovation the services are being held in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall. Preacher 10.30 and 6.30 Rev. H. Wall. . .</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street, Prescher. 11,30 and 6.30 Arr. W. Arnold, Morning subject " The voice of nature" ; evening subject "A Miracle of the past." Baptist Church, Latchford, preacher, 10.30 and 6.0 Rev. J. F. Matthews, subjects : morning," Branded slave " ; evening," A Christian triumph."</p>
        <p>JOHN BULL'S" SUCCESS. At the Chester Carnival on Wednesday night the second,prize for fancy costume and decorated machine was awarded to Master H. Hall, of Warrington, who appeared as "John Bull "—a character which gained him a prize at the recent parade at Warrington.</p>
      </div>
