      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>This Church is at present undergoing renovation and the services are being held in the Wycliffe Hall,Bewsey- street. Last Sunday Mr. Egerton Crookhall, of Cheshunt College, preached to large congregations. Local Preachers' Association.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Wesleyan Churches on Sunday services were held on behalf of the Local Preachers' Mutual Aid Association which does a very commendable work in assisting local preachers who, through old age or other causes, become unable to work. At Bold-street Church the preacher morning and evening was Mr. Stapleton, of Altrincham, while at Bewsev-road the pulpit was occupied by Mr. J. A. Binks, of Manchester. There were good congregations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a very hearty meeting of this Society on nn,lay in the liVinwick street Co-operative Hall. In he absence of Mr.Healey. who was indisposed, the hon. secretary (Mr. B. Farrar), presided. Mr. J. Dean, of Stockport spoke upon "Talents." As Peter said to the lame man "Silver and gold have I none, hut such as I have give I unto thee." so there were gifts of greater value than money. He impressed on his hearers the valne of consecration of voice and heart, time and service, to God. If any had a good and beautiful voice, let them give it to God and His service, like the young lady in Sheldon's book gave her voice and time to the pleasure of the people, and was the means of bringing 111P0 and women to Christ in thousands. Their daily hobbies could also be used for the good of others. Even the beautiful garden with all its glorious buds and flowers, could give untold pleasure to all who beheld it. AU bed one or more talents. Let them use those gifts for the good of their fellows and the glory of God. Mr. R. Mills and Mr. J. Owen also took part in the meeting. Mr. Halesansr with good elect, " The storm " and "The raft." Mr. W. H. Pa.vton Mus. Bac. presided at the piano. The Secretary gave a hearty welcome to one new member, and announced that Li 13s. had been eceirod for the organ fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SHOPEREAKING IN SANKEY STREET.</head>
        <p>SHOPEREAKING IN SANKEY STREET.</p>
        <p>John Ilmnberstone, described on the charge sheet as a general labourer, last from 3fanchester, was brought up at the Police Court on Tuesday, charged with break- ing and entering the lock-up shop of William Sherrin, barber and tobacconist, Sankey-street, and stealing five razors, of the value of 17s. Gd. The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner was a stranger to Warrington, but he had reason to believe he was a ticket-of-leave man. After he bad stated the facts of the case he should ask the Bench to grant him a remand to enable him to make inquiries into the prisoner's past conduct. At ten minutes to eleven on Tuesday night a neigh- bour of Mr. Sherrin's came to the Police Station and made a certain communication to Inspector Smith, the result of which was that that officer went to the rear of some lock-up shops in Sankey-street. He examined several of them, and when at the back of Mr. Sherrin's be found the back door open. He went inside the shop and found the prisoner endeavouring to conceal him- self behind a door. He examined the man and found in his possession five razors. The prisoner said to the officer: "Oh, I'm fairly caught. I took the razors to cut the lock of next door. 1 thoneht I might get some money there." That, added the Chief Constable, was a singular use to put the razors to. He asked for a remand for a week. The application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Fire Brigade News.</head>
        <p>Fire Brigade News.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, the Warrington Fire Brigade, accompanied by the Health and Fire Engine Committees, went to the Sankey Sanatorium to test the water supply. A good supply was obtained from Sankey Brook, and the practice was in every way satisfactory.— The annual competitions of the National Fire Brigade Union (North-Western district) were held at Darwen,on Saturday. Messrs. Crosfield's brigade were prominent competitors and tied for a second place in the hydrant drill. In the steamer wet drill Nelson won the first prize and Crosfield's brigade Vie second, Southport coming ire third.</p>
      </div>
