      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>RE.Y-NA.RD IN SEARCH OF A SUPPER. The fox went out on a wintry night, The trees were bare, the moon shone bright, ".1 am ver-y, var-y hungry," said he, And what I'm to eat I really can't see." --Yes, the poor fox was very hun-grso; he had been to the farm-houses round, and had tak-en what he could, but the far-mers' wives had fast-en-ed the win-dows of the lar-ders, and there was a boy watch-lug the poul-try in the yard, and a man with a gun read-y to shoot the fox who had been such a thief.</p>
        <p>So Rey-nard did not know what to do, and he was ver-y hun-gry in-deed. "I can't get any hares, and there are so few birds left," said he. "I should be ver-y glad Or a scrap of food of any kind." He was sit-ting look-ing up in-to the trees, when sud-den-ly he saw two birds fly-ing a- long. • Wilat kind uf birds can these be?" thought he. If it were not so late in the year I really, think I should say that they were phea- sants. '</p>
        <p>The birds were wood-cocks, and one was say-jug to the oth-er— " All our friends were shot by the sports- men, and we a-lone are safe."</p>
        <p>"Yes," said the oth-er, "but we are not safe yet, nor shall we be un-til te shoot-ing sea-son ends."</p>
        <p>"1 am ver-y tir-ed," said the first one; "we will stay and rest here."</p>
        <p>And Ihey were both go-ing to set-tie down when they saw the fox, who was look-ing up, at them. "No, no, this will not do," said the wood- cock;," there will soon be an end of us if we go near that fox. How hun-gry he looks! We must go on a lit-tle far-ther. And a-way flew the wood-cocks.</p>
        <p>" Ah," said Rey-nard, "that hone is gone, and I am more hun-gry than ev-er." He ran slow-ly along through the wood and a-cross the high-road, where he stop-ped, for in the mid-die of the path he saw a lit-tie par- cel. He sniff-ed at it-and turn-ed it o-ver, and out came a good piece of ba-con. The fox gave a cry of joy. "Now," said he, I shall have a good sup- per." And he car-red the ba-con home to his den.</p>
        <p>A STRANGE ANGLER. I know a little pool Where the water's bright and cool, And the merry speckled troutlets oft are leap. . _</p>
        <p>ing; There so quietly I stay nue, all heedlessly they play, And I often think they fancy I am sleeping. And when the weather's fine At times I take a line, And from the bridge I idly let it dangle; • Bat no hook is there below me, Fir the fishes seem to know me, _ . . _</p>
        <p>And I haven't got the heart for them te angle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SUMMER FAG.</head>
        <p>SUMMER FAG.</p>
        <p>Bile Beans Restore Vigorous Health to</p>
        <p>an Ailing Housewife.</p>
        <p>Summer heat induces languor and lassitude where the vital functions are not up to concert pitch. Bile Beans are a safe and potent vegetable medicine, which set stomach and liver right, and keep them healthy and vigorous. Mrs. Maria Coles, who lives on the Stour- bead Estate, Blackslough, South Brewham, Bruton, Somerset, says : " For 17 years T suffered from ill-health. I had adult and languid feeling which made me so unwell that I could not do my housework. Even after I had had a night's sleep I was so drowsy in the morning that if I I sat quiet a few minutes I would drop off to sleep. I lived at Druley until foar years ago, but although the country there is more open than bete I had the same drowsy feeling, and all work was two much for me. " I continued in this state for two years after I came to Blackslough, and tried many medicines without getting much relief. Then I read in " Lloyd's News- paper how persons enduring suffering similar to mine had been cured by Bile Beans, and I determined to try them. I bought some Beans and took them for three or four weeks.</p>
        <p>"The result was that I found they had done me a great amount of good. lam much better now than 1 have been for years, and can do my housework and help in the garden. I wake up in a morning now feeling quite fresh—quite a contrast to the drowsy feeling I used to have." Bile Beaus for Biliousness are the right medicine to keep handy in the home. They are purely natural in composition, and cure Nature's ills quickly and permanently. Of all chemists at Is. 10. or 2s. 9d. per box (triple size).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>;- BOWLING.</head>
