      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Examiner, Spturda3T,.August 25,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Spturda3T,.August 25,</p>
        <p>"Yes, he refused. He spoke about'the sscraS- nese of the gift and all manner of nonsense of that sort, as if it could matter to a stone image Ny ho wore the necklace for a few hours. In short, I wish to wear that necklace to-night; ram deter- mined to do so. The Rajah protests he loves me 4 you do the same. He refused my request; will you prove your love what you profess it to be?" "In short," said Keaton, "I am to enter the Temple of Hanuman and abstract the necklace for you to wear to-night? Have you thought what the consequences to both of us may be ? " "I only think that you do not love me, or you would not asked me the question," replied Golabee Mookta, with a disappointed air. "But the Rajah would recognise it instantly," objected Keston. "That is just what I wish him to do! He would not do anything beyond that; an Indian host never offends his guests. To-morrow the necklace could be returned to him by me with a little note of thanks." "It would certainly produce a sensation when it became known," said Keaton, who, in spite of himself, was fascinated by the daring plan which the lovely girl had conceived. "Your friend Smithson is close at our heels," said Golabee Mookta, as the sound of a horse's hoofs fell close behind. . "You shall wear the necklace to-night," laughed Keston. "I swear it." "And you will find me on the terrace at mid- night if you should, by any chance, want to ask me anything," said Golabee Mookta with a glance from her full, dark eyes that filled Keeton with hope. She touched her mount lightly with her riding-whip and waved an adieu to Keston, who felt unaccountably angry with his friend for coming up then, although he managed to avoid shewing it.</p>
        <p>1906.</p>
        <p>"Five minutes on the terrace first!" pleaded Reston. "I have earned my reward!" he added _ _ _</p>
        <p>GOlabee Moolta was not disinclined to agree to the request; and the two went out upon the terrace, beneath a lofty verandah hung with great tropical creepers. The night air was heavy with the scent of tropical flowers; below the 'verandah stretched exquisite pepul trees ; further, still, the cool outlines of the far hills formed silhouettes against the moonlit sky, while on the verandah itself, a dove, disturbed from its slumbers, flitted, ghostlike, here and there, cooing for its mate. Keeton poured forth the torrent of his love into the ears of Golabee Mookta, to which the imperious beauty listened, toying with the diamond necklace the while, her eyes turned to the distant hills as thillagli too demure to look into those that were bent upon her. The confession of love was over, and Keston waited for Golabee Mookta's reply. "You have gratified my wish," the beautiful girl began. "This necklace--" The sentence was never finished. As Gplabee Mookta's hand indicated the glittering gems, some- thing black and agile, leaped heavily and suddenly upon her, clawed at the necklace and was gone with its booty before the startled Keston could recover from the horrified cry which came from Golabee Mookta's lips. Keaton snatched upsthe girl, who had fainted, and carried her into the hall, where hie cries quickly summoned assistance. Not only was the necklace gone, but blood was trickling from half-a-dozen wounds in Golabee Mookta's neck and face.</p>
        <p>All that afternoon Keston busied himself with planning how to accomplish successfully the strange task he had so willingly accepted. E'as knew that the Rajah had a great desire to wed Golabee Mookta, and he had been in India sufficiently long to be able to count the cost to himself of taking part in a scheme for the purpose of irritating a Hindia on a matter of such moment as his religious scruples. Still, if Keaton were resolved to marry Golabee Mookta, as he was determined to do, she had given him his opportunity. But how was he, a hateful Feringhee, to pask unmolested in and out of a temple which would, no doubt, he reflected, be thronged with worshipers? Even if he did so, was it possible he could lay hands on the treasure and bear it away to Golabee Mookta? Besides, once the Rajah heard of the engagement between the Eurasian and Keaton' would he not— Stay k; one plan at a time. If he could but get the neck- lace and Golabee MoOkta wore it that night, she would accept him. That was as far as the infatuated man's mind cared to travel for the time being. Consequently, he concentrated his attention upon a plan to get the necklace. Keaton had in his employ a shikaree. who frequently accompanied him on tiger - sh ,oring expeditions, and determined to confide the whole matter to him. Mooly Doon, the shikaree; had been saved from a tiger by the sahib's promptness, and was grateful for the same, which, for a Hindu, was remarkable. He listened to Keston's story, expostulated, implored' finally he came over to the sahib's way of thinking, especially as ten pieces of English gold shone warmly against his brown palm, and then found their way into an old piece of rag in which Mooly Doon wrapped anything that the gods sent his way. When the hot day disappeared and night fell with a cool breeze, suddenly, as it does, Keeton and Mooly Doon sauntereci by the Temple of Hai-Inman. No one would have guessed that the taller one of the pair was an Englishman dressed in the guise of a Hindu. After one or two attemnts to pluck up the requisite courage—for he had no mean task in hand—Keston parted with Mealy Doon, who had his own share in the twofold drama to play, and joined a knot of worshippers entering the temple. On the threshold Keeton, doing as the others did, put off an outer wrap, which the chill night air had demanded, and then went forward, his heart palpitating wildly, but his brain and nerves steady. A great image, made of reddish stone, towered before Keaton, in the dimly-lighted temple, and when his eyes became accustomed to the gloom, he made out every limb and feature of the horrible mcnkey god. It was raised upon a carved base of stone, to which was fastened the varied gifts of the des ot3es of Hanuma.n, and among them, most con- spicuous of all, glittered and scintillated a necklace of large diamonds—the object for which he had entered those sacred precincts. Before the idol stood a pujaree, or priest, half naked, his body smeared yellow, while round him, in a great rough semi-circle, were prostrated a hundred or more worshippers. Keeton, as he too, flung himself down with his forehyad touching the flags, of the temple, could make nothing of the pujaree 's words. He was only conscious that precious minutes were slowly passing, and that the lengthy harangue was likely to last till the hour for the ball or later; in which case Go:abee Mookta, if she went, would not wear the Rajah's diamonds. Had his ally, Mooly Dnon, s forgotten his part? - Ths shikaree, so far from forgetting, was watch- ing and waiting. He understood the harangue, and knew the moment when it could best be inter- rupted. After Keaton entered the temple, Mooly Dram did so too, lurking near the entrance and, watching his chance. Suddenly, from the temple entrance, a loud cry of "Deen! deen!" (religion', religion!) arose from Mooly Doon's lips, and at that outcry, the summons whio no Hindus ever yet neglected or f iiled to obey, the then prostrating pujaree, worshippers and all, rase from the floor and ran from the temple, each clutching what weapon he had, madly anxious to be even with the Feringhee infidels, against whom the cry was raised. It was Keston's opportunity. That, night Golabee Mookta, dressed for the ball, her carriage waiting outside, was walking impatiently up and down hex duir. This English lover of hers was no better t,h,n the rest. He had turned coward! The great di ,mond necklace-- " Salaam!" said the'voice of her Hindu maid at that juncture. "A package has just come. Will it be opened now or to-morrow, when the Rosy Pearl has more time? " Golabee Moolda snatche'd the package from the wondering woman's hands, and eagerly unfastened its wrappings. The Englishman s love had not failed her, for her eyes. fastened eagerly upon a mass-a:4 white, glittering stones! "It is my new necklace, home from the jeweller's just in tima " explained Golabee Mookta to the Hindu woman, and she clasped the necklace about her neck, glanced several times at the result inahe long mirror, laughed a little laugh' of triumph over 1 the -Rajah, and went to her carriage. In When Golabee Mookta was announced at the. ball, the Rajah. as he came forward to, greet her, kept his countenange well when his glknce fell uponl the diamonds gliikring upon the bear throat. I , He recognised them instantly, and determined that , the wearer should be his. If at any moment his love had unconsciously lagged out of memory of i STILL WELL IN ST: HELENS. the past, the mad daring of Golabee Mookta I In this issue we again report the confirmation of a -effaced all thought except of her. He was courtesy statement long since made public. The cordial way in itself, and she, flushed with the triumph and the which this St. Helens resident spoke after four years is effect of the sensation she undoubtedly, produced, ' the best proof any of us could desire to have of the -.vas more gracious to the Rajah than ever before. permanence of his good fortune. She felt, too, that something was due to him, and "Although it was over four years ago that Doan's allowed her host to claim two dances with her. backache kidney pillscured me,l bare kept all right over Keeton, on the other hand, could hardly restrain since, lens. "The"S slklr. Samuel Twiss, 26, St. Paul's-street, St. his anger. Certainly- Golabee Mookta, when they Hemedicine did me lasting good and I haven't needed any more fora long time now. danced together,. formally thanked him for the i loan of the necklace which he had secured, but that asTfhoellolwetste:_r i‘nmweharicsh Nr is irsl:. You uas told us rewelco ohis u welcome th publish is was all. She refused, absolutely, to give him more this letter if you care to, and I. hope it will be the than a single dance, and smoothed over the difficulty means of others bearing of DORII'S backache kidney • by claiming her freedom that night till the mid- pills and being cured as I have been. night hour, at which time. she _promised, if he I suffered very much with pains in the lower part would take her to the supper-room, to give him the of ray back before I used Doan's pills. The pains were which his few minutes he implored her for, and on so bad at times that after I had walked a little way I fate would turn, had to stop and rest. I "Until I began with Doan's backache kidney pills Whatever may have been the reason,' Keston nothing I rried did me much good. From the first I certainly, although he was a dancing man, did not Alt that these pills were helping me and gradually the enjoy the Rajah's hall. He danced with his pains went right away. lam quite cured now, and partners in a perfunctory war, that brought him have only -Doan's pills to thank. (Signed) Samuel sundry indignant glances, wh4h he was too much •Twiss." absorbed to notice. All he was conscious of really Doan's Backache Kidney Pill are two shillings and etlol s -usst e reteht, e was the amazing beauty of Golabee MoMookta';and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and the more he thought of her, and the more ho' ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores, or post free, directxrd.fstretore, L m Foster-McClellan sv Do forget Co.,Cnoo S, rg e Wt watched her lithe-figure threading the mazes of the oco magnificent ball-room, the more wildly,improbable full name, Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. it seemed to him, in spite of everything, that she would accept him. As to the Rajah, lie seemed in high spirit, and , met Keston's involuntary scowls as they happened ;. Warrington P.S.A. to whirl their various partners past each other, The weekly meeting of the Warrington Society was with 'an unperturbed and almost a questioning held in the 00-operative Hall, Sankey-street, gr. R. glance. 1 - Srinner-tit Nelson presided. Mr. William Walker, who has de came, however, at last, and. trt.' Keston's immeasurame be saw uombee recently returne,d to England from India, spoke of the Mookta evidently refusing the Rajah's offer to , manners and customs of the natives, and pointed out take her in to supper —an honour that no on else that only Christianity could meet the needs of the would have demurred to. Keaton moved swiftly _people. Mr. Pownall was the accompanist. Mr. W. across the ball-room, cut the Rajah's pleadings Light took part in the meeting. short, zi.nd secured Gclabee Mookta himself—to the , former's evident annoyance. • ,</p>
        <p>Keeton's friend, Surgeon-Major Smithson, was among the first of the guests to be attracted to where he was trying futilely to staunch the blood with a handkerchief. A minute's examination, coupled with Keston's explanation, gave Smithson the clue to the whole occufrence. Golabee Mookta's wounds were dressed, and when she had sufficiently recovered, she was removed home, while Keaton, debarred entrance, watched the night through out- side the house, where Smithson, coming from a second visit to his patient, found him. "The worst!" ejaculated Smithson, in answer to Keston's eager inquiries. "It depends what view one takes of it. From a man's point of view, it is very little; but from a woman's, it is different," "How ? In what way ? " asked Keaton, chafing at his friend's nonchalance.</p>
        <p>"Merely a case of a few wounds which, in al; probability, will soon heal, but they will leave Golabee Mokta's beauty spoilt, for there will be some pretty scars remaining. I'll tell you what it is, Keston; you don't understand half the import of what has happened. The Rajah does, however, and he has a shrewd suspicion of the whole affair." "What does he suspect P.l-not that I care!" added Keston somewhat inodnsequently. "That diamond necklace belonged to the Rajah's former wife," said Smithson, "and Golabeellookta Bet you on to steal it—yes, I said steal,' because it is the exact word for it—from Hanuman's Temple. The fact is, the Rajah has a good many friends, and sycophants, doubtless too, and your movements are well known to them," "Very well; pray finish," urged Keston. "Having decided to wear that necklace, Golabee Mookta got it by that meats; and you think that one of these horrible monkeys which are so pampered in India and are a nuisance to everyone, stole it fiihm her ;/but you do not realise the whole of the story. The Rajah assured me that no treasure ever given to Hanuman's Temple can be stolen for long—the priests train the mmkeys, who infest the teuyle,, to guard the treasures it contains!"</p>
        <p>"'Ana, in'short, you think----" began Keaton. "The Rajah firmly believes that a certain monkey of the temple, which is known for its almost human intelligence, fellowed you from the temple, and kept you close company, more or less; till it came upon you and Golabee Mookts, who was wearing the neckhme, which the brute consequently snatched and made off with."• "The idea is incredible!" said Reston. "I don't believe it for a moment."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps not," said Smithson reflectively; "but when I add that the Rajah was informed within an hour of Golabee Mookta's dreadful experience on the verandah, that the diamond necklace had been restored to lianuman's Temple by some means, you may draw what can only be the rational conclusion; wh.ch the Rajah does. ' "My dear Smithson," said Reston, after a pause, "you say that Golabee Mookta will only lose her beauty. That is to her an irreparable loss, you think, perhaps; lam going to marry her though, all the same! If that brute had not appeared fpr another minute: I should have been accepted by her, I swear it! When she is well, I will try again. The Rajah may have his own views about the diamond necklace; but I have mine as to whom Golabee Mookta laves—and it is not the Rajah!"</p>
        <p>..... • • A still stranger event, however, proved the falsity of Keston's conclusion. Golabee Mookta recovered from her injuries, but she never saw Keston again. Whether she wished him • to remember her or not as she was before the extraordinary occurrence here related, he never knew. Certain it was that from that day Golabee Mookta never again appeared inA public, nor was she seen again by rival or friend. Moreover, the Rajah soon after left Lucknow for a palace he had fsr distant, and there were persistent rumours which at last reached Keston's ears that in the seclusion of the Rajah's palacp, unlike in most Eastern potentates' abodes, there was, and is yet, but one wife—a woman with one cheek, and one • side of her neck disfigured witlimanv distinct scare. [Tim ESD.</p>
