      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TREASURE OF AN EASTERN GOD.</head>
        <p>THE TREASURE OF AN EASTERN GOD.</p>
        <p>BY CHARLES J MANSFORD, iluthor of "Shafts from an Eastern Quiver, "Under the Naga Banner," "The Adventures of Mark Paton," "Kali and her Blue Diamonds," Ac.</p>
        <p>"I wish you would take my advice and leave Golabee Mookta to her maiden meditations and not try to do the impossible," said Surgeon-Major Smithson to his companion and friend, Lieutenant Keeton, as the two rode side by side through one of the narrow, crowded streets of Locknow. _</p>
        <p>"But I don't see that it is impossible," objected Keaton. "Besides, I'm sure Golabee does treat me a little differently from her crowd of male admirers." "Of course she does," ejaculated Smithson. "You are her latest fad; rnmd you are not her latest victim, too!" Smithson's voice grew serious as he spoke, then,' seeing a surprised • look upon his companion's face, which was quickly followed by one of deep resentment, he added thoughtfully: "My dear boy, I am a score of years older than you are, and my advice is and may be of use if you will but take it. I grant you that Golabee Mookta has a name that is a charm in itself; it means the Rosy Pearl or something of that sort; I believe. She has also beauty of a remarkable kind, and knows how to make the most of it. Her mother was an Englishwoman, and her father a high-caste Hindu, so I've heard, but lam no advocate sof such marriages even in the second degree, as yours would be if she gave you her hand. Her Eurasian blood is, however, a small matter, though it accounts, no doubt, for the strangeness of her character. What does matter, though, is the fact that every man who has hitherto set his affections upon her has come to grief." "Perhaps I am not such a fool as some of them have apparently been," said Keeton, feeling for the ends of his budding moustache, and speaking with evident annoyance. "Well, I suppose each of the others thought that," replied Smithson. "Certainly her, latest victim, Gregory, was as fine a man as ever came out to spoil his liver in this beastly climate. II nu want an object lesson go and read his epitaph the churchyard up in the hills. He took his own life, you know, because she refused him after giving every sign of deep affection for him. Then there was Carson; you knew him as well as ? aid.</p>
        <p>"I think, if you don't mind, we'll drep-tni subject," said Keston. "The road seems to be widening out, and I'll just go on ahead and catch Golabee up; I've something to say to her," he added, almost shamefacedly, and setting his horse at a canter soon reached the side of the subject of the two men's discussion.</p>
        <p>"So you've managed to tear yourself away from your friend at last;", said Golabee Mookta, as the Lieutenant rode his horse by her mount. "What an agreeable conversationalist he must be. When we started homeward from the picnic you were begging for a few minutes with me, and yet I have been riding quite alone, and your friend's conversation proves so attractive that you have not noticed the fact before."</p>
        <p>"We've been riding through so many of them interminable crooked ways," apologised Keaton. "I'm sure, had I known our host, the Rajah, 'would have left you for one moment, I would have cut Smithson and his gossip immediately. It was only as the street widened---" "That you thought of me," interposed Golabes Mookta. "How very kind of you!" "Don't, for , Heaven's sake, be sarcastic,' implored Keeton, "at least, not just now. You know, you must know, that life to me is a misery with- out you. Won't you make up your mind and say ' Yes ? ' I know I'm not so wealthy as the Rajah who is in love with you, of course. But, after all, I can satisfy most of your wishes so far as money will do so. And I don't see how a man--"</p>
        <p>"Who is twice your age, twenty times as wealthy, and a Hindu into the bargain, can love me half as much as you say you do? No! yet you are jealous of him and of every man who ventures to say a civil word to me." "If you would only say 'Yes,' " implored Heston, 'that would remove .jealousy and every- thing else on my part. If there is anything I can do to prove to you that I am consumed by your love, only exprees a wish and it shall be done, but don't torture me by leaving me in this wretched state of uncertainty." _</p>
        <p>Golabee 3lookta was silent for a minute. An elethant with a howdalt on its back came in the, opposite direction, and Keaton fell behind to allow it to pass. When he regained her side she seemed disinclined to renew the subject of their conversa- tion. At last his efforts to continue it were success- ful. Golabee Mookta raised her riding-whip sud- denly and pointed to a strangely carved, temple a,) few yards ahead of them. "Do you know. Sir Knight," she said, with a smile that made Keston's heart leap as be saw it, "what that building is ? " "One of the innumerable temples that one meets at ; every twist and turn'of an Indian city," Keston answered lightly, as lie glanced indifferently at the building indicated. "Yes, it is a temple," agreed Golabee Mookta, "but it is rather a famous one. Would you like to know why?" "I am only too glad to hear the sound of your voice," Keston answeied gallantly, although inwardly he was chafing at what he thought was a mere subterfuge on the iniperioua beauty's part to avoid resuming their former,otopic. Little did ha imagine that the Hindu temple was destined to be as closely connected with his fate as it was. "You flatter me," said Golabee Mookta lightly. "But as you are so pressing for an answer to your question, perhaps that temple may be the means of proving to me what your protestations are really worth, and who knows but what I may answer your question as you would like it answered." She spoke the last few words in an undertone as if addressing herself rather than Keston, but he caught her every worii, as his answer indicated. "Only give me something, anything, to do to shew my love for you and it shall be done," he said eagerly, then added thoughtfully. "But I don't see what I can do se far as that .temple is concerned."</p>
        <p>"Then I will tell yots," said,t3olabee Mookta, speaking low but distinctly. "You know that the Rajah gives a ball to-niat? " •</p>
        <p>- "Of course ; we are both invited," he replied promptly. "You are invited yourself," the said, "and you guess that Jam." "I know very well that the ball is practically given in your honour by the Rajah. however much fie may seek to disguise the fact," Keston answered with a pang of jealousy. The Rajah's ostenta- tious display of his great wealth was one of the causes of,Keston's jealousy of him. "Well, your guess is correct enough," said Golabee Mookta; "so far as the fact irries that I aril invited. As to the wherefore of the ball, the reason you give is .so flattering to me that I will not discuss it. At all events I shall be there. It is difficult to be original at such affairs, one's efforts soon fail but I have an idea for the ball to- night and you, can help me, if you rove use, as you say you do. Will you promise? " "Anything," said Kesiou. "You shall have your reward, if you do," Golabee , Mookta replied. - "To-night t You will say Yes ' to-night?" Keston asked. the blood mounting to, his forehead it the bare thouo-ht of such bliss. .</p>
        <p>"I will say Yes ' to-night, this very night, you fond, .foolish \boy, if you; carry out successfully ivhat I am going to ask." "TeWine what it is, and consider it as good as done," said Keaton firmly. Golabee glanced at the, ITindus thronging the street in whirdi theY, were before answering, as if to make sure no nne was listening. "That temple which I piloted out, is.the Temple 0 Hanuman, the monsey god," said Golabee Mbokta, ane paused.. Keaton was silent; he waited thoughtfully for her to resume.</p>
        <p>"Hartman has tunny worshippers and priests who keep his temple and feed Ate monkeys which infest it and the whole town.. Many treasures are said up in this temple. Two years ago, when his wife died, the Rajah who iA to .be our host to- night laid at to :feet of Hanuman's image her diamond necklace, which was the envy Of evpry woman of caste in India. You are listening? “ciogi:v," saki Keaton, who learnt for the first time that the Rajah had bestowed such a gift on Efanuman'i temple. "I asked the Rajah to-day to grant me nerrniss</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN, No doubt you are all anxiously waiting to hear the result of our Flower Show Competti- tion. I am pleased to tell you that this has been most successful, and I am delighted with some of the lovely flowers that have been sent. 'I have no difficulty, however, in deciding to give the firs,t prize to Lillie Coates, for the remarkably fine sweet peas; I really think these are the .finest I have ever seen, and I congratulate Lillie on being able to grow such very good specimens. Perhaps she will kindly tell me where she obtained the seeds, and how she mauaged to get them so fine. The second prize I must award to John Northcott, although this member sent use a most lovely box of flowers, not all intended for the competition. I consider his mignonette well worth a prize, and I have great. pleasure in sending him the second. I must also thank him for the flowers he so kindly sent me. The pansies sent me by George Ernest Coates, I am very pleased to say, are deserving of the third prize. I am yery fond of pansies, and these were very nice ones; also this member has been very good in trying hard to win prizes. He has been most persevering, and now he is rewarded, for I have sent him a very nice prize. Let this be a lesson to you all: If at first•you don't succeed, try, try, again.' I shall now hope to receive letters from the winners of the prizes, telling me that theyi are pleased them; I shall then print the letters, se.th.at all my nephews and nieces may see that my prizes are weil worth trying for. The prize for the essay on "How I Spent Bank Holiday has been awarded to Margaret M. Press, and I hope she will be pleased with it. Perhaps next year this member will join our Flower and Vegetable Competition, which I intend holding. I hop,e you will all send me stories and pieces of poetry for "Our Corner " during the holidays,. as I feel sure you will have plenty of time but remember that any member sending me a contribution written in pencil will have no chance of a prize.</p>
        <p>I must say- I think Lucy Fitzpatrick very clever at writing poetry, and I hope one day to_be .able to award her,a prize,</p>
        <p>_ I will say good-bye nOw, wishing you may all have most enjoyable lukidays, from Your • affectionate, UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a very funny Essay on Henry VIII., which I hope to see in print, because I think it will make our readers laugh.</p>
        <p>HENRY VIII.-A SCHOOLBOY'S ESSAY.</p>
        <p>Henry VIII. wad' the greatest widower that ever lived. He was born at ANNO DOMINI, in the year 1066. He had 510 WIVES, beeide WOMEN AND CHILDREN. _</p>
        <p>The first one was BEHEADED, ana EXECUTED. The second was R., She never smiled again. The greatest man in this reign was LORD WOLSEY. He was called the "BOY BACHELOR," being born at the AGE OF 15, UNMARRIED. Had he served his WIFE as diligently as the KING, she Would not have deprived him of his grey hairs. Henry VIII. quarrelled with the POPE because he called him " FIO THE OFFENSIVE,' "DANDY LION," and • other unpleoasant appetites. He also quarrelled with, the Monas- tries. He pulled down. BARNS and built GREATER, and the last state of that man was worse than *e first: He died on the field of the cloth of GOLD. His horse trod on an HOT CINDER; and there was weeping and gnashing of teeth. In this reign the BIBLE was trans- lated into LATIN by TITUS OATES, .who wa.F 'ordered by the KING to be chained up in the CHURCH. It was in this reign that the DUKE OF WELLINGTON discovered .AMERICA, and invented CT.IIrFEW BELL to prevent fires in THEATRES. There was also a great fire in LONDON, CALLED THE BLACK DEATH, and after the fire came an EARTHQUAKE, and after the Earthquake a still small VOICE.' HENRY VIII. WAS SUCCEEDED BY HIS GREAT GRANDMOTHER, THE BEAUTIFUL AND ACCOMPLISHED MARY QUEEN OF SCOTS, SOMETIMES CALLED LADY OF THE ' LAKE, OR THE LAY OF THE LIST MIN- STREL.</p>
        <p>-.id afterwards I 'ae REVOKED.</p>
        <p>From your ever-loving Nephew, ERNEST HA.RROLD.</p>
        <p>ncle Ned,—l started my Holidays 1 and I am enjoying myself very mu, . will see by my essay what a lovely ti aad on Bank Holiday. Next week one of ters and a cousin of mine are coming h, a holiday.. We are having lovely weatl .e.• Hoping you and all my cousins te well, I remain,</p>
        <p>Your loving little niece, MARGARET MARY PRESS.</p>
        <p>HOW I SPENT LAST BANK HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>It was a glorious day last Bank Holiday. The sun was shining brightly, and the heat was intense. Two Flower Shows were to be held near our town, one at Ascot, the seat of Mr. Leopold de Rothschild, the other at Woburn. In the afternoon, about 2 o'clock, I started with two of my sisters for the show at Ascot. There were, of course, a great many brakes to convey people to the show, but we preferred to walk, and arrived at Ascot about 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>First of all we went in the Needlework Tent, and everything there was very well done. Then we went in the Flower Tent, and the flowerra were simply lovely; the ferns also bad been very well attended to. We visited the Vegetable Tent next; the potatoes, beans, and rhubarb were the best specimens exhibited here. Fruit also was very good, especially cooking-apples, gooseberries, and currants. After that we walked round tq where the amusements were being carried on. There were motor-car round- abouts and switchback roundabouts. I went first on the motor-car roundabouts, but I did not like it, because they did not go round fast enough for nie. Afto that I went on the switchback roundabouts, and it was lovely; you go round and round and up and down at. the sathe time. I liked it so much that I went on again. There were also cocoanut shies, but I did not have a try, as I am not a very good aimer. ,</p>
        <p>Then I went to look at some actors and clowns; they were very amusing. A conjurer performed a very good trick; ,how he managed it I do not know. It was this: He showed the audience a British flag, then he showed them a foreign country's flag; after that he tied the . two together in a lover's knot. When he had rolled them up and put them in a glass, ne showed the pepple another flag, which he screwed up in his hand, and about a minute afterwards he opened his hand and it was gone. ge then took the flags from out of the glass and unfolded them, and there tied be- tween them was the flag he had screwed up in his hand. But this was not all. He rolled them all up in a bundle and then asked a man if he would see dial the knots were securely tied, and pretended to throw the flags \ to the man, and there instead of the three flags was one big Union Jack. Don't you think that was a very good trick, Uncle? After that my sisters' aNcl myself had tea, and then we were very sorry to find that it was time to go home. We arrived home about 8 o'clock, after having spent a very enjoyable time.</p>
