      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SOMEONE SEES YOU.</head>
        <p>SOMEONE SEES YOU.</p>
        <p>I know a little saying, That is altogether true. My little boy, my little girl, That saying is for you. '4* !Tis this, 0 blue and black eyes, And grey, so deep and bright, e No child in all. this careless world • 3. ever out of sight"</p>
        <p>)ToTaatter whether field or glen, Or city's crowded way, C Or pleasure's laugh or labour's hum, Entice your feet to stray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Morrie Preece, Ernest Preece, Alice -Brother- ton.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l send you a piece of poetry, which 1 wrote myself; also my contri- bution for the "How' I ,`:'pent Bank Holiday" Competition. The poetry is called " The Snow- drop's Lesson."</p>
        <p>I remain, your loving friend, LUCY FITZPATRI HOW I SPENT BANK HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Owing to an accident to my knee, I could not go out anywhere on Bank Holiday, so I determined to enjoy myself at home, and this is the way I did it: After breakfast, my two brothers and I played croquet, for we have 'a. nice large lawn at the back of the house, and as this was a new set, we played till nearly dinner time, taking rest between the games in a large ".soldiers'" tent that we have. After dinner, one of my brothers went for a bicycle ride, while the other one and I tread books and eat sweets at/the same time. We had tea in the garden, and polly, the parrot, whose cage was hanging under a tree, talked and barked and whistled, till we could .1 hardly talk at all, for Poll interrupted us In everything we said. After tea my father and I potted some flowers - for the conservatory; they were red, pink, and white geraniums. We finished •up the evening by a giune of crouquet and glasses of lemonade, and I think we have all enjoyed this Bank Holiday, just as much as if we had gone out somewhere.</p>
        <p>L. G. F.</p>
        <p>THE SNOWDROP'S LESSON.</p>
        <p>Said the snowdrop to the evergreen, As it came up into 'sight, And a tiny spike shot through the ground, "I feel so blithe and bright;</p>
        <p>For it's so damp, so wet and cold, Living underground; But soon be a lovely flower, By some young children found.</p>
        <p>Besides, I want to see that thing, That thing they call the sun." Beware Jack Frost," said evergreen, "For he's a crafty one."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ITURALOSSIP IP THE CLESBEVIAY.I.</head>
        <p>ITURALOSSIP IP THE CLESBEVIAY.I.</p>
        <p>Dustloss Roads.</p>
        <p>Cyclists will hail with satisfaction the aims of the Dustless Roads Association, which has been formed in Birmingham. The trouble is mainly attributable, says the programme of the new organization, to the fact that the roads in many districts are made or repaired with materials which are easily converted into dust. Our efforts are, therefore, chiefly directed towards the elimina- tion of this fault, by bringing to the notice of the various road authorities concerned the means by which improvements may be economically effected; by organizing in individual districts where such feeling is strong the public demand for the adoption of these methods • by disseminat- ing information as to what has been and is being (lone throughout the country, as experimental or permanent work, with the object of securing (lostlessness of roads; by initiating legal pro- ceedings wherever the law affords redress ; by the f.lrmation of influential local committees in those districts where such assistance is desirable; and, ill certain cases, by contributing to the cost of the desired alterations. Sufferers from ' the dust nuisance are advised to communicate with the Association without delay, —giving full particulars of their grievances to the Secretary, Mr. C. F. Vaughan, at 150, Edmund Street, Birmingham. Travelling Light.</p>
        <p>Not a few tourists of experience, who prize the simplicity- and freedom associated with a bicycle holiday, have cut the Gordian knot of the luggage question by adopting one or other of the following methods: Before starting one or 'more parcels, containing a change of underwear, bcc., may be addressed to towns which it is proposed to visit</p>
        <p>during the tour. When these pdiceis are in due , course claimed, the discarded articles can be similarly despatched home by parcel post. A ,second plan, which leaves the tourist absolutely free to change his route as wind, weather, or inclination may dictate, is to purchase such articles as may be needed, when and where may be convenient on the way, and to post home the cast-off raiment as before. Either of these plans, which involves little expense, allows the cyclist to travel with the minimum of impedimenta on his machine, and thus to extract the utmost pleasure from his daily riding. , An Ancient Chronicler.</p>
        <p>Probably few cyclist's of to-day are familiar with the name of Karl Kron; the American cycling enthusiast of the early eighties, who being afflicted with the furor scribendi, indited a bulky volume entitled 'lO,OOO Miles on a Bicycle.' Published by subscription in 1887, after being on the stocks for several yearst the book is now only to be discovered in cycling libraries, and must be regarded as a curiosity. Although 8,000 subscribers were secured beforehand, the critics on both sides of the Atlantic were angered at the writer'sr egotism, when the completed work appeared. As a monument of wasted effort Karl Kron's book forms amusing ' reading to-day, dealing as it does in minute detail with bygone bicycles, early tours, cycling institu- tions long since decayed, and a mass of contem- porary cycling lore rendered useless by the changing hand of Time.</p>
