      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Trailers and Variable Gears.</head>
        <p>Trailers and Variable Gears.</p>
        <p>The cycling editor of the Field ' has expressed disappointment after experimenting with a three- speed gear on a bicycle used in conjunction with a trailer. It seems that the gears chosen were 57, ,70, and 89, the middle or normal gear of 70 being the rider's favourite for ordinary riding. It was found that when the trailer and its •passenger were added, it became impossible to rush slight) ascents on the normal gear; while when the lo'w speed was switched into action the pace fell away to a cra.Fl. Hence the disappoint- ment. The remedy would appear, to consist in the selection of a different combination when trailer work is in contemplation: For -example, 52 65, and should laiwely overcome • the objection. The low gear could then be used for crawling up irksome rises, the moderate normal for ordinary work, and the high kept in reserve for wind-assisted travel on the level, or on downward grades. It must be recognized that the driver of a trailer, like the rider of a heavy machine, is in a sorry plight when opposing a wind. 1 This Year and Next.</p>
        <p>With the end of August lye have reached the close of the cycle trading season, and as regards the volume of business already transacted the current year is expected to compare most satis- factorily with the past. It is stated that approxi- mately half a million new bicycles have been made and sold, and faint-hearted traders fear that the public demand for more may require time to .recover itself. The optimists, however, are con- fident that thousands of non-buyers during 'O6 will want to renew their bicycles in 'O7, that many more will be keen to change their present mounts for lighter machines, embodying some fitment that has fascinated them during this year, and that, what with new converts to the pastime, the rising generation ever recruiting the cyclists' ranks' and the evergreen enthusiasm of the club. men, there is little occasion to be downhearted anent next season's prospects of trade.</p>
      </div>
