        <p>Gladys: "He is such an impulsive lover ?" Edith: "If a girl shows him the least encou- ragement he runs!"</p>
        <p>"Is it not lovely when hushand-and wife are always of the same opinion?" "Certainly; only it makes such a great difference whir opinion it ie."</p>
        <p>• "Do- you boil your drinking water at home?" queried the Chicago man. "No," replied the man from Cincinatti. "We pulverize and then fry</p>
        <p>Edith: "So Ethel is or:sowed to that million- aire; isn't he too old?" -Gladys: "Oh, no. She feels sure he will live until the ceremony."</p>
        <p>Knicker: "What will become of their chil- dren?" Backer: "They will be taken from the seryants of the father, and brought up by the servants of the mother."</p>
        <p>Smith (who has been abroad): "Is young Huggins still paying attention to your daugh- ter?"- Jones: 'No; they are married pow. ' * *</p>
        <p>"You talked all through my solo," said the musician a little resentfully. "Oh, that's all right," said the self-sufficient sage; "I wasn't saying anything you would care about hearing."</p>
        <p>Dolts Hope: "Are you prepared for anything that papa may say when you ask him?" "Yes, dear, or anything he may do. I have taken out a five-thousand dollar accident policy." •</p>
        <p>"Why ,isn't that astronomer as famous as ho &amp;monks to be ?" asked one scientist. "Because," answ.ered the other, "he iststs on using his telescope instead of be imagination."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Siimson "I thought you were coming home early to punish Willie for letting that 'lie?" Slimson: "I was, but I had to stop at the City Hall and swear off my taxes."</p>
        <p>Lady (engaghig cook) : "Why did you leave your last place ?" .Br.dget Maloney: "Whoi, mum, the mistress said she cudn't do Without me, so I came to the conclusion that Oi was worth mom than she was givin' me, and Oi lin at</p>
        <p>* *</p>
        <p>"The at,ar seems to have, plenty of diamonds and automohlies," whispered the sweet singer ; "dtey say she is well fired." "Ah !" chualed ithe comedian," I suppose she is what we would call a flied star."</p>
        <p>Marketer: "How much are strawberrles ?" Dealer: "One dollah a box." Marketer: "'Give me some good sour pickles—they cost less and taste about the same."</p>
        <p>"Yes, indeed, he's the homerest man in public IPfe to-day. Haven't you ever 9een him ?" "No, but I have seen earicratures of him." "Oh, they flatter him... You should eee him." ,</p>
        <p>"No man :;13gu.illy unta he is convicted. You must always remember that." "Yes, but this fellow is a member of the Untted States Senate."</p>
        <p>Kind Lady: ."Have you ever'had educational adcantages, little boy?" Raggecl Kid: "Nope, but I've 'had mumps an' measles an' hoopin' cough. Don't want notbing else."</p>
        <p>Uncle Joeiah : "F:T3t time you ever milked a cow, is tit? Well, you do.,it a thunderiie egight better than meet city fellers do." Visitkng Nephew: "It seems to come natural, somehow. I've had a good deal of practiice with a founitalin- pen." * • *</p>
        <p>The Wfe : "Mat 1-9. A?" The Husband (wearily) : None whatc:ilr,r.' The Wife: " Were there no servamtF, in the intelligence office?" The Hueband (Aaclly): 'Late of them; but they had all worked for 1.16 before." •</p>
        <p>"You're rather a young man to be left charge of a drug.store, " saM the fussy customer, "have you any diploma ?" "Why—er—no, sir," replied the drug clerk, "but I've something jusl, as good)"</p>
        <p>"Do you think that the corporation men are going to rim the government?" "No," ans- wered Senator Sorghum, "I don't think they will give their time to it so long as corporations pay so much larger salaries than the govern- ment."</p>
        <p>• • •</p>
        <p>- "Bobbie. why do you blame all the nattglity things you do on to your little brother?" "Why not? Mamma eays he's too rnall to punish."</p>
        <p>"Is this a fast colour?" asked the man who wanted three pairs of socks for a quarter. "Sir," replied the salesperson blandly, 'that colodr is so fast that, if rt ehould start to run, it would inevitably be arrested for exceeding the speed Limit."</p>
        <p>"Charlie promised that on the day he married me he, would stop drinking." "Did he keep 'his promise?" "Oh, yes. Re -didn't take a drink all that clay."</p>
