      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Prefix Reverend.</head>
        <p>The Prefix Reverend.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A Liverpool paper of the 4th inst had a small paragraph on what it called a "Mammoth Seaside Service' to be held on the evening of the following day (Sunday) on the sands at New Brighton, by Mr. Aked of Pembroke Chapel in that city. Some of your readers, ' will no doubt, remember that this pastor's public renunciation of the prefix reverend led to some corres- pondence in the "Examiner" recently. What effect Mr. Aked's example has had upon his professional brethren in and around Liverpool I do not know; but I regret to say that Warrington and neighbourhood appear to have only produced one follower in the person of the Baptist pastor of Latchford, who seems to drop the obnoxious prefix in his weekly preaching announcements. So far as my experience enables me to judge, I should say that most professing Christians are pleased with the titles of their ministers, thinking that by con- ceding them they are doing honour to God, and con- ferring a special importance upon religious services. While, on the other hand, not a few believers repudiate them altogether,on the ground that they cannot be other- wise than offensive to Him as they contravene the teach- ing and example of His beloved Son,and create and foster a false position among men. A little more honest and independent criticism is, therefore, not only desirable but necessary. I suppose there is nothing upon which our preachers of all denominations pride themselves more than upon their knowledge of the Bible. They served a long apprenticeship to its study, and are still sup- posed to be" searching the Scriptures." I am bound, therefore, to assume that they cannot be ignorant of the important fact that there is neither precept nor precedent for the titles they usurp in the whole com- pass of the oracles of God. In Old Testament times, Moses and Aaron, the Hebrew priests, and the prophets were all especial servants of Jehovah, and by Him appointed to their several callings. But not one of them was, or ever claimed to be, authorised to assume an attribute to God ! And the New Testament is full of examples conveying the same truth. From John the Baptist to the beloved John who wrote the Revelation —the Lord's forerununer, and the Lord's anointed messengers were only known to men by the names they inherited from their fathers. And even the Scribes and Pharisees, who were famed for "making the Law of God of none effect by their traditions," had not yet learned that they were reverend men! But, to crown all, Jesus the founder of Christianity, who should be the exemplar of all who profess to be His followers, not only had neither prefix nor aflix to His name, but actually condemned the use of such to His disciples for all time. And here we are face to face with the serious ques- tion,where did the designations reverend,right reverend, very reverend and most reverend come from ? Not from heaven certainly. For if God had intended that ministers of religicn should dignify themselves in this way we may rest assured that The Book" which was written by inspiration for our learning would have contained both precepts and precedents for the practice. But, as we have seen, the Scriptures are absolutely silent upon the subject, the only reference to it being Christ's prohibition of the use of titles by His servants. And surely it will not be contended that modern ministers, who can produce neither revelation nor call- ing from God, are greater than His chosen servants of old that they should be thus exalted ! The truth is, Mr. Editor, clerical distinctions "are of the earth, earthy," and seem to emanate from a rather low plat- form too. In fact we might safely lay responsibility for them upon human pride and presumption, qualities which are known to be especially offensive to Him who is no respecter of persons. And their object is to ele- vate their wearers above their fellow men, and thereby attach, to, them an importance which they do not possess. Take a case in point. John Smith in ordinary attire is only an ordinary member of society. Hut when he passes muster by a Bishop or a Conference, and dons a clerical bib and tucker a marvellous change is alleged, to take place. Mr. Smith is then authorised to adorn his name with one of the attributes of the Almighty, although there is not a particle of evidence to show that be has been metamorphosed in any way. Nor is this all. Should the worthy priest, through patronage or other cause, attain to the highest plat- form of his profession his name will then be several degrees more reverend than that of God himself! Could human presumption and audacity go higher than this ! And does it not point, like a finger post, to the coming time when a man shall declare himself to be God ! It would not be fair to charge all the responsibility for clerical titles to the clerics themselves much as they are benefitted by them. So long as men and women with an open Bible in their hands admire and condone the imposition, and would probably resent its " dis- establishment "I fear there is little hope of clearing it out of the churches and chapels at present. Still, one must not dispair. The subject is, undoubtedly, exercis- ing some minds as it never did before, and one may reasonably hope ere long to see others follow the example of Messrs Aked and Matthews in renouncing a prefix to which they never had, and never could have, any title whatever. Yours truly, August, 1503.</p>
        <p>I-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>The Thirteen A Side Game.</p>
        <p>A CURIOUS BLUNDER.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday the football season opens in earnest and for the ensuing eight months the winter game I will be in full swing. More interest than usual is being taken in club prospects in Northern Union circles owing to the revolutionary change of thirteen a side in all matches instead of the time-honoured fifteen. There is plenty of justification for the altera- tion, and confidence is felt in most quarters that it I will be a success. Last season was probably I the worst from a playing point of view I in the history of the Union. Important games deter- iorated into mere scrummaoes and scrambles, but now with four men less on the field, amended touch rules ' and a " play the ball " rule, bright open play should be seen in every match where teams are willing to' play the game. Reforms were absolutely necessary if the Northern Union was to be kept alive, and it is to be hoped that at last a style of play will be evolved capable of maintaining the interest and support of the public. A curious blunder was made by our contemporary, the " Guardian," on Wednesday in its review of foot- ball prospects, and those Northern Unionists who read the column must have opened their eyes pretty wide in astonishment. The writer based his remarks on the entirely erroneous assumption that Warrington and other Northern Union teams are going to play three threequarters and seven forwards. He says it " May be taken for granted that the system of three threequarters will be the order," and proceeds to argue from this false standpoint. We should not have thought that there was a Rugby enthusiast in Warrington so ignorant of the actual facts as the writer in our con- temporary proclaims himself to be. The playing of three three-quarters and seven forwards has never been contemplated by the Warrington Club in connec- tion with the thirteen a side game. In fact, on the contrary, they were —as originators of the reduction in numbers—strongly opposed to a suggestion by the Oldham Club that three three-quarters and seven forwards should be played, A plebiscite of the leading clubs was taken and the majority were against any alteration of the usual back formation. The Union Committee have it is true, left the matter open, but there is not the least likelihood that more than two or three clubs in the League will attempt any varia- tion from the style of play which has been in vogue for years past. The Warrington Club will certainly not depart from the four three-quarters system, and only six forwards will, of course, be played. No one has anticipated anything else and how the "Guardian" comes to state the opposite so definitely is incomprehensible. They are quite "at sea,",how- ever, as a little inquiry in official quarters will at once prove. With the reduction in the number of players, the prospects of the Warrington club may be considered to be improved. There should be no difficulty what- ever in selecting six forwards of the highest class from the playing strength of the club. Preston has trained 'down over a stone in weight during the close season, and should be all the smarter for it, while men like Boardman, Thomas, Harmer, Swift, Heath and Belton are reported to be fit as a fiddle. Berry should fill the full back position to the I satisfaction of all, and with men like Hockenhall, I Brooks, Lambert and Lees to choose from there should! be little difficulty in getting the right pair of halves. In the threequarter line things are hardly so satisfac- tory. Fish—the captain for the season—is quite sound once more and likely to be at his best when the season opens. Isherwood too can be relied upon, but Dickinson is still weak in the knee, and hardly likely to be of much service. In his place there is Taylor, a sound centre, but on the right wing there is a limited selection. Harris is not the man he was, and Jordan hardly approaches first-class yet, so that the authori- ties are in somewhat of a difficulty. An effort is being made to import a class man for this important position and if the right man is obtained there should be little fear of Warrington not going far in the new League. More is expected of them than formerly, now that there are four positions to be ' fought for, and if Warrington cannot claim one of the four at the end of the tourney there will be much disappointment. Several practice games have been held during the toast few weeks, and the men have turned out well. Moreover, they are falling rapidly into the new style of play brought about by the prohibition against direct kicking to touch, and the " play the ball " rule. Some very last, open practice games have been wit- nessed, and when the selected side goes to Leigh on Saturday next it should be able to give a good account of itself against the League Champions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>NUMEROUS LOCAL SUCCESSES. On Monday the lists of successful candidates in the Oxford Local Examinations were issued by the secretary to the delegacy. The senior candidates numbered 4,645 against 3,646 last year, the juniors 7,630 as against 6,960 and the preliminary 3,567 as against 3,616. The total number examined was 15,842 as compared with 14,222 last year, and 11,497 certificates were awarded as against 0.732 in 1905. Of the candidates examined there passed 3,078 seniors, 5,565 juniors, and 2,855 preliminary. The following are the successful senior candidates in this district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FIONOIJRS LIST.</head>
        <p>FIONOIJRS LIST.</p>
        <p>Framed on the aggregate work of the candidates, and containing the names of those who obtained honours, and the title of Associate in Arts. First Class (81 candidates).-49th (bracketed equal with eight others), H. 0. Adams, Boteldr Grammar School, Warrington. Second Class (102 candidates).-10th (bracketed with five others), R. Wilcox, Boteler Grammar School. Third Class, arranged in numerical order under the several centres (516 candidates) : Widnes.—E. Chadwick, Secondary School. Warrington.—R. Lee and A. Wright,Boteler Grammar School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PASS LEST.</head>
        <p>PASS LEST.</p>
        <p>First Division, framed on the aggregate work of the candidates, and containing the names of those born on or after July 1,'1886, who satisfied the examiners and obtained the title of Associate of Arts ; arranged in numerical order under the several centres (2,094 candidates). Widnes.—G. H. Ackerley, G. L, Hayes, J. Gibbs, E.. Hankey, and M. Roscoe, Runcorn Institute School: E F. Dickson, H. Skellon, W. Thomason, and T. F. Whitfield, Secondary school, Widnes; H. A. Burgess and B. Sephton, Pupil Teacher's Centre, Widnes. RESPONSIONS.</p>
