      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>PENKETH SHOW.</head>
        <p>PENKETH SHOW.</p>
        <p>Creditable Horticultural Display.</p>
        <p>The members of the Penketh and Sankey Window, Cottage Garden and Cage Bird Society are to be con- gratulated on the success of their fourteenth annual show which took place in a field off Warrington-road, Penketh, on Saturday afternoon. The exhibition was in every way a credit to the district and attracted large numbers of visitors. The plants and flowers made a very pretty display, the grouping being most effective. The following sent exhibits, but not for competition : —Mrs. Robert Garnett, Mrs. C. E. Caird, Mr. H. Hope, Messrs. Caldwell and Sons, Knutsford, and Mr. d. Higgs. In each case the collection was an excellent one, and much admired. The arrangements had been admirably carried out by a Representative Committee under the Chairmanship of Dr. Murray, with Mr. A. 0. Clay as treasurer. Mr. J. Hampson officiated as secre- tary of the cage bird section, Mr. A. Seed as general secretary, and Mr. J. Foden as assistant secretary. The judges, who discharged their duties in an able and expeditious manner were :—Flowers S:c., Messrs. Clarke and Foden ; birds, Mr. Houlton. The President, said he thought they would all agree with him when he said that the exhibits were improving every year. (Hear, hear.) This year they had gone in for a new arrangement in having one large tent in which to hold the exhibits, and also new arrangements —which had been somewhat difficult to make—had been entered into with regard to the flowers to suit the tastes of all present. There were one or two new groups, One was a group for table decoration. Unfortunately, there had only been three competitors, but next year, it was hoped there would be three times as many. New classes had been formed for farmers' produce, but no farmers had come forward. He expected they were all too busy in getting in the harvest. Another new classx which had been taken advantage of by one exhibit° was an open class for market gardeners, nurserymen, and florists. Perhaps next year they would have more exhibitors of that description. The show was inaugurated to encourage the villagers to go in for nice gardens, and he thought the excellence of the flowers and vegetables ' was a proof that they were progressing in their garden culture. (Applause.) It was hoped that more would go in for the exhibition than had done in the past. There were two other outshoots, viz., creepers and tree-plant- ing. The class for creepers was a new one, and there had been several competitors this year. Tree-planting was growing all over the country, and they hoped some time during the season in Penketh to plant trees on the Recreation Ground, and it was hoped that those who I could put up trees in different parts of the village would! do so, because there was now no fear of them being killed by the fumes from Widnes. He had much pleasure in asking Mr. C. E. Parker to open the exhibi- tion. (Appluse.) Mr. Parker said they could all love flowers, which , could not be in any way monopolized. (Hear, hear). I No one could make a ring in flowers, and keep them to themselves. (Applause. If they had no gardens they ' could have flower boxes, and if they could not have the flower boxes, they could go into the county lanes and I see " the lilies of the fleld,hovr they grow,' and in spite I of all the culture that had been given to flowers, those ! that nature herself produced in the country lanes and fields were very difficult to beat. They could see forms as perfect, and smell perfume as sweet, as anything that ' was grown in a garden. In art, literature, the drama ; and national history, flowers formed an important part. The duty he had to perform that afternoon was to ' declare the show open. He was surprised to bear that , that was the fourteenth show, and tie could endorse what Dr. Murray had said—that he thought this was the best show they had yet held. It was certainly very creditable to a small village like their own. He had never seen such beautiful potatoes as those on exhibi- tion that day, and he was pleased that the number of , exhibitors was considerably greater than ever. (Applause). On the motion of Mr. J. B. Aitken seconded, by Mr. Ambler a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Parker.</p>
        <p>_ During the afternoon the Penketh Prize Band and a glee party from Widnes, pleased all present by an excellent musical programme. The following is the PRIZE LIST. Gardens, &amp;c.-Large cottage flower garden: 1, A. Seed; 2, W. Dutton. Small cottage garden with green- : 1, J. Walters ; 2, A. Birch. Medium cottage garden without greenhouse :1, R.Holcroft ; 2, J. Leigh. Small cottage garden without greenhouse : 1, J. Hall ; 2, G.. Leigh. Best arranged and cultivated kitchen garden : 1, J. Goulden ;2, Turner. Best arranged and cultivated allotments, Sankey : 1, D. Bonehill ; 2, Turner. Best arranged and cultivated allotments, Penketh : 1, G. Leigh ; 2, J. Carman. Climbing rose: I, A. Birch ; 2, T. Carrington. Anipelopsis : 1, T. Dunbavin. Creeper of any kind: 1, A. Bowyer; 2, J. Hampson. Plants.-Three window plants in pots or baskets : 1, J. Leigh. Window plant (foliage) : 1, C. Bromley ;2, F. Smith. Window plant (flowering) : 1, W. Jones ;2, A. Knowles. Four cottage greenhouse plants: 1, A. Seed ; 2, J. Walters, Pot of Miura auratum : 1. J. Hampson ;2, J. Walters. Cottage garden greenhouse : 1, A. Seed. Stand of plants and cut flowers from cottage garden and greenhouse: 1, J. Walters. Cut Flowers.-Six cottage greenhouse roses : 1. J. Waive ; 2, W. Dutton. Six roses (hybrid perpetuals) : 1, J. Carrington ; 2, W. Dutton. Six roses (hybrid teas): 1, .1, Carrington ; 2, J. Davies. Six roses (tea, scented or noisette) : 1, W. Dutton. Three roses (hybrid per- petuals, 1, J. Carrington ; 2. J. Goulding. Three roses (hybrid teas) : 1, .1. Goulding ; 2, J. Davies. Asters : 1, S. Moss ; 2, A. Seed. Carnations : 1, H. Bingham ; 2, W. Dutton. Picotees : 1, A. Skelhorn ; 2, B. Bingham. Violas, self colour: 1, H. Bingham ; 2, J. Carrington. Violas, any variety : 1, D. Bonehill ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WARRINGTON POLICE SPORTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL MEETING.</p>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. ,</p>
        <p>Openshaw—Melville.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Earlestown are to be hailed champions of the West Lancashire League and they certainly deserve the honour for they have played consistently well through- out the season. This, however, is but poor comfort to Warrington who are only one point behind and whose one mistake of allowing themselves to be defeated earlier in the season by Earlestown practically took away their chances of the cup. Earlestown put the finishing touch to their season's record on Saturday by defeating St. Helens—a victory which just made them champions, for had they been unsuccessful Warrington who easily accounted for Prescot, would have gone to the head of the League. The Warringtonians were in splendid form and trounced their opponents most severely. Prescot were all dismissed for the miserable total of 45, the highest individual score being 20 by J. P. Preston. Cresswell and Callon shared the bowling and gave the batsmen a lively time, the professional capturing 6 wickets for 18 runs, while Callon took four for 25.</p>
        <p>Warrington carried all before them and in twenty minutes the Prescot score was passed. The innings realized 190 for 9 wickets, the feature of the day being the fine innings of J. Callon. He hit out in remarkably vigorous style and compiled 87 which included 14 fours and two threes. Richardson again gave a good account of himself, knocking up 33. Wright and Cresswell were each responsible for 27, the professional carrying out his bat.</p>
        <p>The championship of the second division has not yet been decided and the position is a peculiar one. At present Warrington are at the head but a match between Prescot and Newton-le-Willows which was stopped earlier in the season has to be re-played at Newton on September Bth and a great deal depends upon the result. If Prescot are victorious they will draw level with Warrington and the teams will have to play off for the championship. If, on the other hand, the game ends in a draw or Newton win,Warrington will be the champions. It is interesting to note that Prescot were the winners last year.</p>
        <p>-- If Warrington 2nd have to meet Prescot in a final struggle for the leadership they should be more than able to hold their own,for only last Saturday they inflicted a particularly severe drubbing on the Prescot eleven. They succeeded in dismissing their opponents for 86 and then knocked up 214 for the loss of only seven wickets. E. E. Woods batted brilliantly and carried out his bat after making 73. H. Dolan played stylishly for his 43 which included nine fours and H. Houghton scored a faultless 43. Thewlis was very effective with the ball, capturing seven wickets for 45 runs. Plinston's two wickets cost 178 runs and Bennett took one for 18.</p>
        <p>Walker's Warrington Brewery team had the satisfac- tion of defeating their visitors from Burton, thanks in a great measure to the bowling of J. Dargan who took six wickets at the very lowcost of 12 runs. J. Peterson (19 not out) was the top scorer.</p>
        <p>Rylands Bros. registered a victory at the expense of the " Guardian " eleven, J. Beardsall being the most successful batsman with 16.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyans accounted for Fairfield by a majority of 20.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Engineers had to bow the knee to Latchford Baptists who triumphed to the extent of 105. Cox bowled splendidly, taking seven wickets for eight runs, while Conway captured two for four. Warhurst was the top scorer with a capital 52.</p>
        <p>J. Dargan, of Walker's Brewery fame, has been honoured with an invitation to join the ground staff at Old Trafford which it is probable he will accept. Dargan who throughout the season has bowled with marked success, is a young player with a remarkable turn of speed, and if he profits by the training which he will receive at Old Trafford, he will doubtless occupy a leading place amongst bowlers of the country. He is also a proficient batsman and on Tuesday assisted Walker's Brewery to a victory over the Liverpool Police. The Brewery were set the task of scoring 100 runs in 50 minutes in order to win and when time was called Dargan was 50 not out, whilst the total score was 110 for the loss of two wickets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-08-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>Leigh Railway have a surprising facility for winning the Lancashire and Cheshire County Association championship. Since 1900 they have secured the honour five times and it would appear that no club is strong enough to wrest it from them. Their position was seriously assailed this year by the Cemetery men but when they triumphed in the semi-final their ultimate success was placed beyond doubt. On Saturday they I met the Chorlton Bowling club in the final and wining I with remarkable ease, became the first holders ot beautiful silver cup presented by the president of the j association, Mr. A. H. Crosfteld, M.P. Through the I generosity of Mr. Crosfield each player will also receive la gold medal to commemorate the victory.</p>
        <p>As was to be expected the Liberal Club team on Saturday revenged themselves upon Wilmington Park for the defeat which they sustained earlier in the season. The match was played on the Bewsey-street green and led by Mr. J.&amp; Ws.rren the local men laid themselves out to win and handsomely achieved their object, with the result that on the two matches, the Liberals claim an advantage of 50 points. Warrington Conservatives were not so succesful as their political opponents and were defeated by the Altrincham Tories by 78 points. In all probability the fact that they were figuring on a strange green accounted for their non-success and when the Altrincham Club next visit Warrington the scores will, it is confidently expected, be reversed. On</p>
      </div>
