        <p>WOMAN S REALM. DRESS—FASHION—HOME, DIANAS ON THE HEATHER. .It will probably' have little effect on the.death- rate among the grouse, but there have never been so many women who have gone to Scotland fully equipped for the slaughter as this year. In other days it was the eustorti for the women to stay at °home/when their lords went to the moors, and to pass the time until the return at nightfall in reading and walking. Sometimes they even would knit and sew, or if they were of advanced views they would golf or fish. But now woman has developed a taste for the thril- ling joy of bringing down a bird on ;the wing, and not even the necessity for 'wearing short homespun skirts •or the fatigue of tramping the uneven and tiring moors can restrain them from accompanying' the men. A• fashionable gunsmith in Bond-street declares tha;, during the past three or four years the ranks of the sportswomen have increased enormously. Feather-weight guns are manufactured for them by the gross, and for weeks before :the Twelfth " they go .to the private practising ground of this firm, and waste , many cartridges firing at elav pigeons. A few manage in the course of a week or two to ac- quire a certain 'amount of skill. but the greater number never succeed in bringing down one of the clay birds. ninally,kfiring when the bird has dropped in the grass. taio,irt. LIGHT COAT. As the summer advances the need of a light coat is felt to be a necessity for chilly days or evenings. Our sketch depicts a useful little coat • which could be carried out in linen or thin cloth or serge, either in cream or any colour becoming to the wearer; though for a coat of this kind it is as well to choose a soft shade, so that it will not clash with the various coloured frocks worn beneath. The upper part of the coat is 13 lain'</p>
