        <p>Cycling a Decade Ago. When the history of cycling comes to be written, the year 106 will assuredly form its most romantic page. A magipal stimulus was infused into every phase of the pastime during that period, which makes the progress of events to-day seem sluggish and feeble by comparison. Cycle racing was at its zenith. - The Wood Green, Catford. and Putney tracks, since effaced, were in their glory. Indoor " racing • flourished awhile at Olympia • and the Agricultural Hall. 20,000 spectators witnessed the (=jaw,' chain matches at Catfoid, when the professicird exponents of the Simpson chain defeated those using plain chains. Arthur Linton' Won the Bordeaux-Paris race and saccutubPd to typhoid; while his brother Tom accomplished 80 miles inside the hour for the first tine in the • • words history. Aided by human pacers, the • amateur F. It. Goodwin compiled his 476 miles in 24 hours at Herne Hill, a record Which st'inds to this day. The Society of Cyclists, Lady Cyclists' r"ssialion, and a host •of clubs since dead then r6bed, while cycling journals galore grew and Prospered on the merry advertisement. An abortive attempt was made to mix the amateur and pro- fessionaloyelisti, while a cycle tax scare.. and the adoption, of bicycles by the Post Office also marked the year. Over fifty American firms invaded the English cycle market. Scores of companies were floated with big capitals, mostly to be overtaken by failure when the slump set in. Fashionable society bought and rode bicycles freely, and amongst the (•onspicuous novelties were the Acatene chainless N:lfety, ths bamboo bicycle, and the Fleuss tubeless tyre—all things of the past to-day.</p>
